WEBVTT

00:04.710 --> 00:08.000
We have no intention of dismantling the regulatory agencies.

00:08.550 --> 00:12.920
However, we must come to grips with inefficient and burdensome regulations.

00:13.430 --> 00:16.720
We will eliminate those that are unproductive and unnecessary.

00:16.800 --> 00:21.020
Tearing down these antiquated walls and granting banks significant new authority.

00:21.730 --> 00:23.640
In a victory for the banking industry,

00:23.730 --> 00:25.600
Congress has approved legislation

00:25.690 --> 00:27.900
which will allow commercial and investment banks

00:27.980 --> 00:32.360
to merge and form institutions of unprecedented size and global reach.

00:35.610 --> 00:41.830
I think it's very important for us not to introduce regulation for regulation's sake.

00:42.330 --> 00:43.910
I do believe in the American dream.

00:45.460 --> 00:48.000
Owning a home is a part of that dream.

00:50.000 --> 00:56.090
We are taking action to bring many thousands of Americans closer to owning a home.

00:56.430 --> 00:59.890
This project not only is good for the soul of the country,

00:59.970 --> 01:02.010
it's good for the pocketbook of the country as well.

01:02.680 --> 01:05.440
The housing market has reached frantic proportions.

01:05.520 --> 01:08.480
It seems that everybody is building, buying or selling.

01:08.560 --> 01:11.400
The Dow and the S&P both breaking records.

01:11.480 --> 01:15.110
Profits soared 93% at Goldman Sachs this year.

01:15.950 --> 01:20.070
Hotshot traders and bankers can expect to take home 10, 25,

01:20.160 --> 01:22.790
and in some cases, $50 million.

01:22.870 --> 01:25.910
Things that are too good to be true, are.

01:26.000 --> 01:27.830
When you see something coming...

01:27.920 --> 01:28.920
The real estate bubble...

01:29.000 --> 01:29.960
- Housing bubble.  - Bubble.

01:30.040 --> 01:31.750
don't put it off.

01:31.840 --> 01:33.590
Take action immediately.

01:33.670 --> 01:36.220
Will there be a soft landing, or will the bubble burst?

01:40.510 --> 01:43.260
The new story, the mortgage meltdown.

01:43.350 --> 01:45.770
This morning in the mortgage meltdown, new numbers showing

01:45.850 --> 01:48.770
that a record number of Americans are losing their homes.

01:48.850 --> 01:52.440
More than 70 mortgage companies have failed in the last few months in America,

01:52.520 --> 01:56.860
and now the biggest of the big companies, the Bear Stearns, the big banks,

01:56.990 --> 01:58.570
are caught holding the bag.

02:01.830 --> 02:05.200
The fifth largest investment bank in the country, Bear Stearns,

02:05.290 --> 02:08.830
is being sold for just $2 a share to JPMorgan.

02:08.920 --> 02:12.130
It's being assisted by the government, which is stepping in to guarantee

02:12.210 --> 02:16.760
some $30 billion in Bear's toxic real estate assets.

02:19.380 --> 02:23.970
You've got to imagine that Hank Paulson didn't think he signed up for this

02:24.060 --> 02:27.600
when President Bush picked him to become Treasury Secretary.

02:27.980 --> 02:31.900
Hank Paulson may now have to undo some of the deregulation measures

02:31.980 --> 02:35.860
that he and his peers on Wall Street pushed through when he ran Goldman.

02:37.820 --> 02:40.820
Treasury Secretary Hank Paulson telling Matt Lauer this morning,

02:40.910 --> 02:44.620
"We're all over it. I've got great confidence in our markets.

02:44.700 --> 02:46.290
They're resilient. They're flexible."

02:46.370 --> 02:51.080
Ali, the truth is that Bear Stearns is only a very small fraction of this story.

02:51.790 --> 02:55.630
Our economy could face far worse than a recession.

02:58.590 --> 03:01.800
In the wake of Bear's collapse, Lehman Brothers is scrambling

03:01.930 --> 03:06.180
to reassure investors about the impact of the mortgage crisis on its own assets.

03:06.270 --> 03:09.020
Insurers of the firm under a great deal of pressure this morning.

03:09.100 --> 03:11.850
Dick Fuld, a 39-year veteran of Lehman

03:11.940 --> 03:15.440
and the longest-running CEO of a major investment bank on Wall Street,

03:15.520 --> 03:18.900
has pulled the firm back from the brink during other crises,

03:19.440 --> 03:21.660
but today, for the first time that I'm hearing it,

03:21.660 --> 03:24.660
investors are questioning his leadership.

03:24.740 --> 03:26.030
- Motherfucker.  - One key question

03:26.120 --> 03:27.740
investors have for Lehman Brothers,

03:27.830 --> 03:30.830
"What's the true earnings power of this investment bank's business?"

03:39.460 --> 03:41.380
- Hey.  - Get up here.

03:41.470 --> 03:43.300
We gotta start stacking sand bags.

03:43.470 --> 03:44.590
What are you talking about?

03:44.680 --> 03:49.770
We're down 21%. On paper, I just lost $90 million, personally.

03:51.350 --> 03:53.690
With investors pummeling Lehman shares,

03:53.770 --> 03:55.440
the big question this morning is,

03:55.520 --> 03:57.520
"What options does Fuld have?"

03:57.610 --> 03:59.940
So far, he has failed to raise new capital

04:00.030 --> 04:02.780
while virtually all of his rivals have already done so.

04:02.990 --> 04:05.160
And now, hedge fund manager David Einhorn

04:05.160 --> 04:07.330
is publicly questioning the accuracy of the fund numbers.

04:07.410 --> 04:08.490
Anything good?

04:09.330 --> 04:13.000
It's a red-tailed hawk, first one this year.

04:13.250 --> 04:14.420
Dick Fuld's on one.

04:14.500 --> 04:16.750
to provide credible answers.

04:16.840 --> 04:18.590
So let's go through a couple of those, uh,

04:18.670 --> 04:20.710
what you call discrepancies, because...

04:25.010 --> 04:27.510
- Tough morning?  - We show top-quality earnings,

04:27.600 --> 04:29.850
- we beat every prediction.  - Yeah.

04:29.930 --> 04:33.730
It's the damn short-sellers. You know the game, short and distort.

04:33.810 --> 04:35.850
They're rumormongers and they're making a killing.

04:36.020 --> 04:37.940
What are your plans for a capital raise?

04:38.150 --> 04:40.520
We're way ahead of you. Now, let me be clear,

04:42.240 --> 04:44.530
we don't need the cash.

04:45.820 --> 04:48.030
You know, it's about perception.

04:48.120 --> 04:49.740
Buffett, Buffett.

04:51.370 --> 04:53.700
We're thinking of reaching out to Warren Buffett.

04:53.790 --> 04:54.790
Great.

04:55.580 --> 04:58.210
You and Buffett go back, right?

04:59.380 --> 05:01.670
Do me a favor and call him.

05:01.760 --> 05:03.340
Tell him Lehman is rock solid.

05:03.720 --> 05:07.130
As Treasury Secretary, I can't advocate for a specific investment.

05:10.720 --> 05:14.890
Hank, you want me to raise capital or not?

05:15.810 --> 05:18.480
Lehman Brothers is a hell of a lot bigger than Bear Stearns.

05:18.560 --> 05:19.900
The market is terrified.

05:20.440 --> 05:23.230
When I was running Goldman, I never even conceived

05:23.320 --> 05:25.780
of asking the Treasury Secretary to cut a deal for me.

05:25.860 --> 05:27.280
Yeah, but the Bear bailout created...

05:27.360 --> 05:28.610
Please don't call it a bailout.

05:28.700 --> 05:33.040
Okay, the very large Bear Stearns purchase assistance package

05:33.120 --> 05:34.830
created an expectation.

05:34.910 --> 05:36.580
Fuld's counting on direct intervention from you.

05:37.040 --> 05:39.080
Buffett's a simple solution.

05:40.750 --> 05:42.090
I think you make the call.

05:42.250 --> 05:44.210
As what? Warren's friend?

05:44.210 --> 05:47.170
His former banker? The Treasury Secretary?

05:47.260 --> 05:49.430
No. Fuld's calling him.

05:49.760 --> 05:51.300
Hopefully he'll jump in and we can all relax.

05:51.390 --> 05:53.680
Relax? We just buried one investment bank,

05:53.690 --> 05:55.350
we got another one that's barely breathing.

05:55.430 --> 05:58.190
This is a confidence game. People lose faith in Lehman,

05:58.270 --> 06:00.020
these banks are gonna drop like dominoes,

06:00.110 --> 06:03.480
and I don't have the authority to do a thing about it.

06:03.570 --> 06:07.110
We're late. We've been late on everything.

06:07.280 --> 06:10.360
Neel's team is working on some kind of a disaster scenario.

06:10.450 --> 06:12.280
We're calling it the "Break the Glass" plan,

06:12.370 --> 06:14.490
what to ask Congress for if your back's against the wall.

06:14.790 --> 06:15.950
Money.

06:17.790 --> 06:19.830
Is what you ask them for.

06:21.290 --> 06:25.130
The problem is, Congress won't move until we've already hit the iceberg,

06:25.210 --> 06:26.630
and at that point, it may be too late.

06:26.720 --> 06:31.590
So you're saying, with the full weight of the United States Treasury behind me,

06:31.680 --> 06:34.930
all I can do is call Warren Buffett?

06:35.640 --> 06:38.770
Hi, Warren. It's Hank.

06:39.390 --> 06:41.560
I understand you've been talking to Dick Fuld.

06:41.650 --> 06:47.400
You know my misgivings, Hank, about investment banks.

06:47.490 --> 06:50.360
As soon as they started trading for themselves,

06:50.450 --> 06:52.070
the risk managers lost control.

06:52.160 --> 06:54.660
I had a very unpleasant time with Salomon Brothers.

06:54.740 --> 06:56.830
That's fair enough.

06:58.330 --> 07:02.210
But we both know that investment banking is a profitable business.

07:02.420 --> 07:05.840
I figure it made you a billion or so, huh?

07:05.920 --> 07:10.010
I gotta believe that if the price was right, you wouldn't just walk away.

07:10.090 --> 07:14.680
You know a good deal when you see one. What say you?

07:14.930 --> 07:17.560
I gotta leave that to your judgment.

07:18.680 --> 07:20.980
People act like we're crack dealers.

07:21.060 --> 07:22.810
Nobody put a gun to anybody's head and said,

07:22.900 --> 07:26.320
"Hey, nimrod, buy a house you can't afford. And you know what? While you're at it,

07:26.400 --> 07:28.440
put a line of credit on that baby and buy yourself a boat."

07:28.690 --> 07:30.950
You heard anything from Buffett?

07:31.240 --> 07:35.490
He's asking for preferred shares at 40 with a dividend of 9%.

07:35.580 --> 07:37.990
We were just at 66. What the fuck?

07:38.080 --> 07:40.040
Maybe it's just an opening gambit, Dick.

07:40.160 --> 07:41.710
Sounds more like a goddamn insult.

07:41.790 --> 07:43.210
- Let me get that, Dick.  - Thank you.

07:43.380 --> 07:45.330
We're at 36 right now.

07:45.880 --> 07:48.750
We haven't been anywhere near 66 in months.

07:48.840 --> 07:50.760
The markets like Buffett.

07:50.840 --> 07:53.630
- His name will push the price up overnight.  - I don't care who he is,

07:53.800 --> 07:58.810
I am not spending $360 million a year for the pleasure of doing business with him.

07:58.890 --> 08:00.020
Real estate will come back.

08:00.100 --> 08:01.640
Koreans have been sniffing around.

08:01.730 --> 08:03.520
There you go. And they won't steal us blind.

08:03.600 --> 08:06.360
I've seen this before. CEOs panic and they sell out cheap.

08:06.440 --> 08:09.440
Right now, the Street's running around with its hair on fire,

08:09.530 --> 08:11.400
but the storm always passes.

08:11.490 --> 08:16.120
We stand strong, and on the other side, we'll eat Goldman's lunch.

08:18.160 --> 08:19.910
So, what do we do about Buffett?

08:20.660 --> 08:23.620
Screw Warren Buffett.

08:25.790 --> 08:27.420
- He passed?  - Lehman dropped.

08:27.500 --> 08:29.750
to 28 and a half this morning. What in the world is he thinking?

08:29.840 --> 08:33.840
He's not thinking. He's delusional. He thought the ask was too high.

08:35.260 --> 08:36.680
He's gotta raise cash.

08:36.760 --> 08:38.600
I told him that.

08:39.930 --> 08:42.100
- I spoke to Greenspan.  - Really?

08:42.310 --> 08:47.560
He says there's too much housing supply. That's the problem.

08:48.860 --> 08:53.440
So we should buy up all the vacant houses and burn them.

09:00.660 --> 09:03.950
Dick needs a buyer, someone with a grip on reality.

09:04.120 --> 09:06.290
Barclays expressed some interest a couple of months ago,

09:06.370 --> 09:08.130
but I don't think the price is low enough for them.

09:09.000 --> 09:11.210
- Bank of America?  - Possible.

09:11.300 --> 09:13.170
Dick's put out feelers there before.

09:13.720 --> 09:15.840
We have to keep it quiet, though.

09:15.930 --> 09:17.930
'Cause if anyone hears that they're trying to sell

09:18.140 --> 09:20.970
Lehman'll be dead within the hour.

09:21.310 --> 09:23.470
The real estate investments are killing the firm.

09:24.140 --> 09:26.390
We have to admit publically we've made some mistakes.

09:26.560 --> 09:28.520
We need to make a senior management change,

09:28.610 --> 09:30.690
signal to the market that we get it.

09:32.230 --> 09:35.400
- Meaning what?  - Meaning Joe Gregory.

09:35.990 --> 09:38.450
Get the fuck out of here.

09:40.030 --> 09:41.830
You are not gonna tell me how to run my company.

09:41.950 --> 09:44.910
Joe hasn't had your back on risk, and everyone knows it.

09:45.000 --> 09:48.330
Erin Callan's gotta go, too. As far as the Street's concerned, she's a joke.

09:48.460 --> 09:52.340
Joe Gregory has been with me for 30 years.

09:53.510 --> 09:57.010
And you want me to kick him to the curb because we had a bad quarter?

09:57.090 --> 09:58.630
It's not just a bad quarter.

09:58.720 --> 10:00.680
Stock hit 23.

10:00.760 --> 10:04.310
Twenty-three. Most of our guys are paid in stock they can't sell for years.

10:04.390 --> 10:07.020
They've lost most of their net worth over the last six months.

10:07.520 --> 10:11.060
You don't stabilize the price, your traders are gonna jump ship.

10:11.230 --> 10:13.020
Selfish fucks.

10:13.110 --> 10:16.990
It's not just bankers and traders, Dick. Clients are pulling their money.

10:17.200 --> 10:19.780
There's gonna be a run on this bank.

10:23.540 --> 10:25.540
Sources tell CNBC that both

10:25.620 --> 10:29.250
Erin Callan and Joseph Gregory are out at Lehman Brothers.

10:29.370 --> 10:31.750
In a statement, the company's chairman, Dick Fuld, said

10:31.840 --> 10:34.750
replacing Gregory with 48-year-old Bart McDade

10:34.840 --> 10:38.090
has been one of the most difficult decisions he's had to make.

10:39.800 --> 10:42.550
Dick, the Koreans just arrived at Sullivan.

10:43.680 --> 10:44.850
Let's go make this happen.

10:45.390 --> 10:47.730
My take is you'd be better off staying here for now.

10:49.560 --> 10:51.350
We need you to be our "missing man."

10:51.900 --> 10:54.320
We'll go in and push the deal as far as we can,

10:54.400 --> 10:57.990
and then we'll bring you in at the end to top it off.

10:58.150 --> 10:59.400
All right, fine.

11:00.530 --> 11:02.320
Okay. Okay.

11:07.910 --> 11:10.540
We are committed to our presence in New York.

11:12.880 --> 11:15.920
But we need to know that the underlying numbers make sense.

11:19.260 --> 11:21.930
We realize how things look.

11:22.010 --> 11:24.220
But our stock is getting hammered by the shorts.

11:24.640 --> 11:26.100
The value is still there.

11:28.100 --> 11:31.560
We just started. Min's excited, but we haven't heard the terms.

11:33.150 --> 11:34.230
Where are you?

11:34.310 --> 11:35.940
I just stepped out to take the call.

11:36.820 --> 11:38.480
Get back in there. Don't be rude.

11:39.780 --> 11:41.570
Okay.

11:41.650 --> 11:44.490
We simply take the assets you don't find appealing,

11:45.530 --> 11:47.660
spin them off into a separate company.

11:49.950 --> 11:52.410
- You don't buy that bank.  - I'm very sorry.

11:52.500 --> 11:54.420
You buy the good bank.

11:57.210 --> 11:58.710
Look, they don't like the real estate.

11:58.800 --> 12:00.130
What do you mean, they don't like it?

12:00.220 --> 12:03.970
Dick, we're making real progress here, okay? Let me stay on it for now.

12:20.030 --> 12:22.150
Min, how are you?

12:22.700 --> 12:24.570
Great to see you.

12:25.530 --> 12:27.120
I don't want to interrupt.

12:27.410 --> 12:29.540
Dick, great timing.

12:29.620 --> 12:32.160
We seem to be in sync on the broad strokes of the deal.

12:32.710 --> 12:34.710
Shall we get back to the letter of intent?

12:34.870 --> 12:39.670
Min, I have to tell you, I think you're making a big mistake here.

12:39.840 --> 12:42.340
There's great value in the real estate assets.

12:43.550 --> 12:45.510
How about we walk through the portfolio

12:46.180 --> 12:47.680
and I'll show you?

12:50.560 --> 12:52.350
We have looked at them.

12:52.430 --> 12:56.020
Then you must have looked at them in the wrong way.

12:56.860 --> 12:59.650
See, I just don't wanna see you leave money on the table.

12:59.730 --> 13:02.360
Perhaps we should take a break.

13:08.320 --> 13:10.450
Min, I'm so sorry.

13:10.540 --> 13:14.660
We have negotiated in good faith. Now all of a sudden we are going backward?

13:14.750 --> 13:16.290
We can work with you on the price.

13:16.370 --> 13:18.210
It is not about the price.

13:18.590 --> 13:22.000
It is about the way you have conducted this.

13:27.680 --> 13:30.640
I'd like to thank you, all of you,

13:30.930 --> 13:34.220
but I don't think we have a structure that works.

13:40.440 --> 13:41.650
What, that's it?

13:41.730 --> 13:43.530
You're just gonna pack up and go back to Korea?

13:57.500 --> 13:59.460
- Hello?  - Neel, it's Dick Fuld.

14:01.090 --> 14:04.710
Listen, you know, if Hank wants me to find a buyer,

14:04.800 --> 14:06.260
you guys need to step up and help.

14:06.630 --> 14:08.930
The Koreans just walked out, okay?

14:09.090 --> 14:10.640
Look, I'm just speaking for myself here,

14:10.720 --> 14:13.060
but I think you need to do what's right for the firm.

14:13.140 --> 14:14.850
Do what's right?

14:14.930 --> 14:16.430
What is that supposed to mean?

14:16.600 --> 14:18.690
If your stock price keeps sliding,

14:18.770 --> 14:21.230
you may get an offer at a price that doesn't look all that compelling,

14:21.310 --> 14:24.270
but you may have to take it just to keep the company intact.

14:24.690 --> 14:26.030
What kind of price are you talking about?

14:26.280 --> 14:27.690
Could be...

14:27.780 --> 14:30.070
I mean, it might be low single digits.

14:32.660 --> 14:36.330
Last February, we were at 66 a share.

14:38.330 --> 14:42.580
Lehman Brothers is not Bear Stearns.

14:42.670 --> 14:46.130
We've got a great business. Real estate's gonna come back.

14:46.260 --> 14:49.260
I am not fucking giving this company away!

14:49.760 --> 14:51.000
While the real estate market has put

14:51.000 --> 14:52.550
put Lehman Brothers shares under pressure,

14:52.640 --> 14:54.720
mortgage giants Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac stocks

14:54.810 --> 14:58.100
are suddenly plummeting this morning, after reports the companies

14:58.180 --> 15:02.060
may need to raise $75 billion just to remain solvent.

15:02.150 --> 15:04.400
Because they guarantee such an enormous percentage

15:04.480 --> 15:05.900
of the nation's mortgages,

15:05.980 --> 15:07.730
there is renewed speculation that the government

15:07.820 --> 15:09.820
may have to step in and take them over,

15:10.240 --> 15:13.620
a possibility Barack Obama has now raised on the campaign trail.

15:13.700 --> 15:16.410
If Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac collapsed,

15:16.490 --> 15:22.580
then probably the financial system would receive such a body blow

15:22.670 --> 15:25.170
that it could be disastrous.

15:25.500 --> 15:29.510
Freddie just dropped another 8%. Fannie's down 5%.

15:30.130 --> 15:34.800
There's no bottom here. We need to do something. Now.

15:36.260 --> 15:37.930
We're working on a proposal for Congress,

15:38.020 --> 15:40.730
see if we can get the authority to put some money in.

15:40.810 --> 15:44.980
I'd rather not go that way, but if we get there...

15:45.060 --> 15:47.770
I think we're already there.

15:49.740 --> 15:52.610
- How much are you gonna ask for?  - A blank check.

15:53.320 --> 15:56.700
It has to be enough of a statement to reassure the market.

15:56.780 --> 16:00.500
They need to think I've got a bazooka in my pocket.

16:00.580 --> 16:04.670
I'm not saying that we use it, but if we have the authority,

16:04.750 --> 16:08.500
everyone who's dumping their shares will calm down, back off.

16:09.380 --> 16:12.050
Okay, if you're going to Congress, why don't we give to the banks, too?

16:12.130 --> 16:14.380
'Cause Lehman's looking like shit and Merrill's not far behind.

16:14.550 --> 16:17.220
We'll be lucky to get them to move on Fannie and Freddie.

16:17.310 --> 16:19.680
We do what we can do.

16:30.900 --> 16:33.360
Secretary, Mrs. Paulson, over here.

16:33.910 --> 16:35.280
Over here, please.

16:38.740 --> 16:39.950
Secretary Paulson, over here, please.

16:40.040 --> 16:41.830
Mrs. Paulson. Thank you.

16:45.080 --> 16:46.540
Mr. Paulson.

16:46.630 --> 16:49.130
Mr. and Mrs. Paulson, this way, please.

16:54.590 --> 16:56.300
- I've got her.  - You should take her.

16:57.930 --> 16:59.760
Right over here, Secretary Paulson, please.

17:01.020 --> 17:02.100
Big smile. Thank you.

17:02.850 --> 17:04.180
That's just what I need.

17:04.270 --> 17:07.310
Press photos of the Treasury Secretary waving the Chinese flag.

17:07.400 --> 17:12.440
Paulson the Communist. The look on your face was priceless.

17:14.400 --> 17:17.160
Hank, I must ask you about Fannie and Freddie.

17:19.910 --> 17:22.160
We have hundreds of billions invested.

17:22.160 --> 17:25.160
You've assured us the investment is safe.

17:25.250 --> 17:28.040
And it is, absolutely.

17:29.420 --> 17:32.550
We're watching a great deal of our money disappear.

17:32.630 --> 17:36.720
Share prices have dropped 60% in the last two months.

17:36.800 --> 17:39.300
Carrying your bazooka around has not helped matters.

17:40.350 --> 17:43.560
Congress gave you the power. Perhaps it is time to use it.

17:43.640 --> 17:47.390
The market will stabilize. We just have to give it some time.

17:47.850 --> 17:51.360
There was an approach last month from Russia.

17:52.480 --> 17:55.240
They have considerable holdings in Fannie and Freddie as well.

17:56.240 --> 17:58.910
They suggested we coordinate, and without warning,

17:58.990 --> 18:00.610
dump hundreds of billions of Fannie

18:00.610 --> 18:02.410
and Freddie's bonds onto the market.

18:05.910 --> 18:07.870
- That would be...  - Chaos.

18:08.830 --> 18:12.000
The amount of debt your country carries

18:12.000 --> 18:13.380
is a terrible vulnerability.

18:19.140 --> 18:22.760
- But you...  - We declined, respectfully.

18:25.520 --> 18:27.600
Even in the US, it seems

18:27.690 --> 18:29.560
the relationship between the government

18:29.560 --> 18:32.360
and private industry isn't so simple.

18:36.610 --> 18:38.240
Was it a threat?

18:38.320 --> 18:41.950
No, no, it was a friendly reminder

18:42.030 --> 18:44.000
that with a single phone call to Moscow,

18:44.000 --> 18:45.990
they can take down the entire US economy.

18:53.880 --> 18:58.470
The book says Fannie and Freddie are a nightmare.

18:58.550 --> 19:01.220
They are in the hole easily tens of billions of dollars.

19:01.300 --> 19:03.550
Another few weeks, they're gonna start defaulting on their debts.

19:03.640 --> 19:04.860
We're gonna have to step in.

19:04.860 --> 19:07.560
I don't see how we can avoid it.

19:10.350 --> 19:13.560
Turns out a bazooka's not that useful unless you fire it.

19:15.650 --> 19:17.690
Lehman's down another 10%.

19:17.780 --> 19:20.320
You are not gonna let me get down a single bite, are you?

19:20.400 --> 19:21.610
This is why I have oatmeal.

19:21.700 --> 19:24.320
Dick is trotting around the world with his hat in his hands,

19:24.410 --> 19:26.160
no one will give him any money.

19:26.240 --> 19:28.330
Korea walked out on him. Mexico told him to shove it.

19:28.410 --> 19:30.540
No one's interested. All the dumb money's already in.

19:30.620 --> 19:32.420
What about B of A? Are they still in the mix?

19:32.540 --> 19:35.170
And Barclays is sniffing around again,

19:35.170 --> 19:36.670
now that they think it's a fire sale.

19:36.750 --> 19:38.550
- That's promising.  - I don't know.

19:38.630 --> 19:42.340
The British, they do a lot of talking. They never close.

19:44.350 --> 19:47.310
- B of A is still our best bet.  - This can't be another Bear.

19:47.390 --> 19:50.350
Absolutely not. They want Lehman, they buy it outright.

19:50.430 --> 19:52.100
But they may need a little hand-holding, though.

19:52.190 --> 19:54.650
They just bought Countrywide. They're spread a little thin.

19:55.110 --> 19:58.280
You may have to give them a break with the capital requirements.

19:58.440 --> 20:01.740
You want me to allow them to raise their leverage

20:01.820 --> 20:03.280
so they can buy a bank that's about

20:03.280 --> 20:05.280
to fail because it was overleveraged?

20:06.950 --> 20:08.950
You got a better idea?

20:09.750 --> 20:12.460
Breaking news just coming in. Sources telling CNBC

20:12.460 --> 20:14.570
that the government has just taken

20:14.570 --> 20:16.840
control of Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac.

20:16.920 --> 20:18.880
Back in July, you remember, Hank Paulson said

20:18.960 --> 20:20.520
that he wanted the power to step in,

20:20.520 --> 20:22.720
but he probably wouldn't use it.

20:22.800 --> 20:24.340
It certainly sounds like Paulson

20:24.350 --> 20:26.340
just pulled the trigger on that bazooka.

20:26.430 --> 20:28.430
Despite the move to nationalize Fannie and Freddie,

20:28.510 --> 20:31.770
the market is still in negative territory across the board.

20:31.850 --> 20:35.020
Investors now focusing their attention on Lehman Brothers,

20:35.100 --> 20:36.880
amid fresh reports that the nation's

20:36.880 --> 20:38.480
fourth-largest investment bank

20:38.570 --> 20:40.280
may be on the brink of bankruptcy.

20:40.480 --> 20:41.900
Rodge.

20:41.990 --> 20:43.490
Chris.

20:43.570 --> 20:45.030
Bring your money today, huh?

20:48.620 --> 20:51.290
Make sure Fuld's not keeping any bad news out of the mix.

20:51.370 --> 20:54.330
It's "open kimono," to quote Dick.

20:54.670 --> 20:55.830
There's a revolting image.

20:59.500 --> 21:02.460
Did, uh, you get a look at the numbers?

21:03.050 --> 21:05.970
The real estate's the issue. They got it marked at 30 billion.

21:06.050 --> 21:10.060
It's worth maybe half of that. Don't look so nervous.

21:10.140 --> 21:11.650
You're about to get a Wall Street

21:11.650 --> 21:13.350
bank for the price of a hot dog.

21:13.850 --> 21:15.580
I know that Paulson told Ken Lewis

21:15.580 --> 21:16.980
that he's not gonna pay for this.

21:17.060 --> 21:18.610
And I don't see how we can do it

21:18.690 --> 21:20.570
unless the government takes some of the downside.

21:20.860 --> 21:22.650
Paulson is posturing.

21:22.740 --> 21:24.780
He'll write a check just like he did

21:24.780 --> 21:25.780
for Jamie Dimon on the Bear deal.

21:25.860 --> 21:26.580
He has to.

21:26.580 --> 21:29.780
He can't lose another major bank in an election year.

21:29.870 --> 21:31.590
Besides, the guy made, like, a half

21:31.590 --> 21:34.290
a billion bucks at Goldman Sachs.

21:34.370 --> 21:37.250
He lets Lehman die? Goldman's biggest competitor?

21:37.330 --> 21:39.000
Makes him look like he's still working for Goldman.

21:39.090 --> 21:41.880
He's just doing it out of a desk at Treasury.

21:44.220 --> 21:46.090
Where are the donuts?

21:47.090 --> 21:49.550
Exactly. If you're the CEO of one of these financial firms,

21:49.640 --> 21:52.220
you've already seen what the government was willing to do.

21:52.310 --> 21:54.770
Why wouldn't you play chicken with them and say,

21:54.850 --> 21:58.100
"Sure, I'll help you out. But you gotta throw in a sweetener,

21:58.190 --> 21:59.940
just like you did for those guys"?

22:01.400 --> 22:04.530
They just assume I'm gonna bail out Dick Fuld,

22:04.610 --> 22:07.570
like I'm the guy behind the elephant with the shovel.

22:07.660 --> 22:09.450
They're all drawing a line from Bear

22:09.450 --> 22:11.450
and Fannie and Freddie right to Lehman.

22:11.530 --> 22:13.370
There's no way the taxpayers are gonna go for another bailout.

22:13.450 --> 22:16.080
Very large purchase assistance package.

22:16.250 --> 22:19.330
We need to come up with a private-sector solution,

22:19.420 --> 22:21.420
get the investment banks to bridge the gap.

22:21.500 --> 22:23.440
Okay, but that's never gonna work

22:23.440 --> 22:25.260
unless they believe that you've drawn a line.

22:25.340 --> 22:28.430
I know you don't like leaking, but let us reach out to the press.

22:34.890 --> 22:35.970
Okay.

22:39.060 --> 22:41.690
Mr. Flowers, there's a call for you.

22:41.770 --> 22:45.780
Bob Willumstad from AIG. He's very insistent.

22:51.160 --> 22:54.620
Mr. Flowers. My people tell me you're the man to talk to.

22:55.040 --> 22:56.450
We've got a real problem here.

22:56.750 --> 22:58.120
Yeah? What kind of problem?

22:58.290 --> 23:01.290
It looks very much like we are about to run out of cash.

23:01.630 --> 23:02.790
AIG?

23:02.880 --> 23:04.880
We've sent over a document. It's a spreadsheet.

23:04.960 --> 23:07.800
It's confidential, of course, but it might give you some insight.

23:08.170 --> 23:10.470
I'm in the middle of Lehman. I'm working with B of A.

23:11.300 --> 23:13.470
Is there any way you could come over tomorrow?

23:13.970 --> 23:17.390
I'll take a look at the numbers and we'll talk.

23:23.060 --> 23:25.230
Mr. Flowers.

23:28.690 --> 23:30.110
Somebody put the TV on.

23:30.450 --> 23:32.860
CNBC's talking about Lehman.

23:37.580 --> 23:40.500
A person familiar with Paulson's...

23:40.500 --> 23:42.500
Treasury Secretary Hank Paulson's thinking,

23:42.580 --> 23:45.290
and he is saying there will be no government money

23:45.590 --> 23:47.750
in the resolution of this situation.

23:47.840 --> 23:50.170
They're saying two things make the Lehman deal different.

23:50.260 --> 23:52.760
The market's been aware and had time to prepare

23:52.840 --> 23:55.050
for over six months. And the second is...

23:55.140 --> 23:57.760
Is that just TV bullshit?

23:57.850 --> 24:00.930
Or is that Paulson sending us a message?

24:01.690 --> 24:02.770
Chris?

24:10.360 --> 24:11.530
Yeah? Sorry.

24:15.030 --> 24:17.150
What exactly does "a person familiar

24:17.150 --> 24:18.450
with the Treasury Secretary" mean?

24:18.660 --> 24:20.700
That could mean a lot of things.

24:21.120 --> 24:22.460
Did one of your staffers do this?

24:22.540 --> 24:24.540
Please tell me someone got fired 10 minutes ago.

24:24.630 --> 24:26.460
The leak came from me.

24:29.000 --> 24:30.380
Hank, a public statement like that

24:30.380 --> 24:32.380
can have a lot of unintended consequences.

24:32.470 --> 24:34.140
We had to send a message to the other banks.

24:34.140 --> 24:35.340
They need to step up.

24:35.430 --> 24:37.390
I don't have another rabbit I can pull out of my hat.

24:39.310 --> 24:42.100
Rodgin Cohen just called me.

24:42.180 --> 24:43.440
The B of A team is sitting there

24:43.440 --> 24:44.940
playing Brickbreaker on their phones.

24:45.020 --> 24:46.230
They don't want this deal.

24:47.060 --> 24:49.000
The deal is hanging by a thread because

24:49.000 --> 24:50.480
the closer they get to Lehman,

24:50.570 --> 24:52.860
the more it looks like a toxic waste dump.

24:53.030 --> 24:56.450
Which is why at the end of the day we may have to help them,

24:56.530 --> 25:00.030
which would be a lot easier if we didn't publicly state that...

25:00.120 --> 25:02.160
We can't, Tim! Legally, we can't!

25:02.330 --> 25:07.580
Legally, we haven't figured out how yet.

25:09.090 --> 25:12.510
I am concerned

25:13.260 --> 25:18.340
we are limiting our options at a time when we have very few,

25:18.430 --> 25:20.100
if any, available.

25:23.100 --> 25:28.730
I want all the CEOs in a room. We've got the weekend.

25:28.810 --> 25:30.900
We lock them in a room at the Fed

25:31.150 --> 25:34.570
and we don't let them out until they have a solution on Lehman.

25:35.110 --> 25:36.530
Your oatmeal's getting cold.

25:36.610 --> 25:39.620
They'll figure it out. They'll all throw in some money.

25:41.620 --> 25:44.190
Tim is advocating strongly that we

25:44.190 --> 25:45.290
reconsider some kind of Fed support.

25:45.540 --> 25:47.710
Tim wants us to pick up the tab on everyone.

25:47.710 --> 25:49.710
I can't be Mr. Bailout.

25:50.670 --> 25:54.010
The Lehman hole is twice what Bear's was.

25:54.090 --> 25:55.540
Investors are pulling their accounts.

25:55.540 --> 25:56.630
You want to lend into a run?

25:58.640 --> 26:00.470
What about Barclays? Are they still interested?

26:00.640 --> 26:03.310
They can't afford the risk any more than B of A can.

26:04.060 --> 26:06.810
I don't doubt your ability to talk anybody into submission,

26:06.890 --> 26:09.560
but it is still a long shot.

26:09.650 --> 26:12.860
And there's a very real possibility that Lehman goes under.

26:12.940 --> 26:15.190
There's a deal to be made here.

26:16.990 --> 26:18.990
I'm gonna make the deal.

26:19.570 --> 26:22.830
With just a little more than an hour left until the market

26:22.910 --> 26:25.410
closes, shares of Lehman Brothers have taken another tumble.

26:25.500 --> 26:29.580
The stock is now trading at a new low, under $4 a share.

26:29.670 --> 26:30.980
And judging from the selling,

26:30.980 --> 26:34.300
there doesn't appear to be a bottom in sight.

26:37.630 --> 26:40.130
So, Blankfein has a speech he's trying to get out of,

26:40.220 --> 26:41.260
but the rest have confirmed.

26:41.350 --> 26:43.550
- Tell him it's not optional.  - Yeah.

26:43.810 --> 26:46.100
Good afternoon, Chairman. Thank you so much for joining us.

26:46.810 --> 26:50.270
- Whose plane is this?  - Uh, that's a rental.

26:50.350 --> 26:51.980
Hank put it on his card.

26:52.060 --> 26:55.020
Sir, you know Neel and Michele Davis, Public Affairs.

26:55.110 --> 26:56.150
Hi. Pleasure.

26:56.240 --> 26:58.440
- This is Dan Jester, our new numbers guy.  - Hi.

26:58.530 --> 27:00.280
I talked him into coming out of retirement.

27:00.360 --> 27:02.870
- Excuse me? Retirement?  - He was with us at Goldman.

27:03.080 --> 27:05.240
Got anybody at Treasury who wasn't at Goldman?

27:05.330 --> 27:06.200
Chairman, just to be clear,

27:06.200 --> 27:09.000
there's no question of conflict of interest.

27:09.080 --> 27:11.790
Hank sold all of his Goldman stock before he took office.

27:11.880 --> 27:14.210
- I wasn't implying anything.  - Sorry.

27:14.300 --> 27:17.380
We're a little touchy about the whole "Government Sachs" thing.

27:18.050 --> 27:19.840
Shall we?

27:23.430 --> 27:25.510
B of A keeps finding more toxic assets.

27:25.510 --> 27:27.810
They want you to reconsider a Jamie deal.

27:27.890 --> 27:29.480
They'll split the first billion in losses,

27:29.480 --> 27:31.480
but they want you on the hook for the next 40.

27:31.560 --> 27:32.850
You did it for Bear.

27:34.150 --> 27:35.940
Greg, let me get back to you.

27:36.400 --> 27:38.690
What, do I have to tattoo it on my forehead?

27:38.690 --> 27:39.990
We are not bailing out Lehman.

27:40.070 --> 27:41.650
Wall Street has a gambling problem.

27:41.740 --> 27:44.530
If government keeps covering their losses,

27:44.530 --> 27:45.830
they never learn anything.

27:47.700 --> 27:49.450
- What?  - I didn't know if you wanted

27:49.460 --> 27:51.530
to keep lecturing me on moral hazard

27:51.530 --> 27:53.830
or if I should just call B of A back.

27:54.540 --> 27:55.550
We need to keep them at the table

27:55.560 --> 27:57.750
until they come up with a private-sector solution.

27:57.840 --> 27:59.420
Tell them to stay with us.

28:01.090 --> 28:02.880
- Alistair, hello.  - Hello, Henry.

28:02.970 --> 28:05.090
Hold on one second.

28:06.600 --> 28:08.560
Chancellor of the Exchequer.

28:08.640 --> 28:12.020
I understand Barclays is one of your potential buyers for Lehman.

28:12.100 --> 28:14.810
My banks are already under a great deal of stress.

28:14.900 --> 28:16.610
I don't want to become overextended.

28:16.690 --> 28:19.150
Lehman is problematic, but there's value there.

28:19.230 --> 28:23.450
We will find a way to make it attractive. Just stay with us.

28:23.950 --> 28:25.110
Talk soon then.

28:25.200 --> 28:26.450
Bye.

28:26.660 --> 28:31.160
We'll keep Barclays in the mix, but B of A is still our best bet.

28:31.660 --> 28:35.210
We've got the CEOs of every major bank coming here tonight.

28:35.290 --> 28:38.340
We have to make it clear that this is their problem,

28:38.420 --> 28:41.840
that we are their advisors and their allies,

28:41.920 --> 28:45.970
but we are not their safety net.

28:47.140 --> 28:48.470
What should we expect from these guys?

28:59.440 --> 29:01.230
Jamie is smart.

29:01.320 --> 29:04.450
He doesn't have as much cash to work with since he bought Bear.

29:06.870 --> 29:08.740
Lloyd's a superstar.

29:09.870 --> 29:11.700
Goldman's the smartest shop on the Block.

29:11.790 --> 29:15.160
And just because I used to be his boss, it doesn't mean he's gonna listen to me.

29:17.250 --> 29:20.090
John Mack, he used to run around the floor screaming,

29:20.170 --> 29:23.510
"There's blood in the water! Let's go kill someone!"

29:24.260 --> 29:26.340
He's a fighter.

29:28.220 --> 29:32.010
Thain was my number two at Goldman. He's selfish. He's a pragmatist.

29:32.100 --> 29:35.350
He knows if Lehman goes, Merrill's next.

29:37.350 --> 29:40.360
Vikram Pandit is the new guy at Citigroup.

29:41.940 --> 29:45.530
No one knows if he's running Citi or if Citi's running him.

30:08.720 --> 30:11.890
Thanks for coming down here on such short notice.

30:12.810 --> 30:16.600
Monday morning, Lehman will no longer be able to honor its obligations.

30:19.400 --> 30:22.230
We have two potential buyers. B of A and Barclays.

30:22.320 --> 30:26.820
Neither will take the company unless someone else finances part of the deal.

30:26.900 --> 30:30.200
The government is not in a position to step in.

30:31.490 --> 30:34.580
I did the last one. You're gonna do this one.

30:34.700 --> 30:36.040
Where's Dick Fuld?

30:36.120 --> 30:38.370
Dick's in no condition to make any decisions.

30:38.460 --> 30:41.460
We're breaking you into three groups.

30:41.750 --> 30:46.380
The first one will try to value Lehman's toxic assets.

30:46.760 --> 30:49.970
The second one will work on some sort of structure for you all to invest in Lehman.

30:50.390 --> 30:54.010
And the third one works on the lights-out plan.

30:54.350 --> 30:56.600
If Lehman files for bankruptcy, we need to figure out

30:56.680 --> 30:58.890
how to contain the damage to the rest of you.

30:59.190 --> 31:00.270
Christ.

31:00.440 --> 31:02.150
We've all seen this coming for miles.

31:02.230 --> 31:05.360
I don't think a Lehman bankruptcy takes down our firms.

31:05.440 --> 31:07.940
What, are we crossing our fingers?

31:08.110 --> 31:13.120
You're asking us to save a competitor. Let's not act like you're doing us a favor.

31:13.660 --> 31:17.370
Gentlemen, gentlemen, you are great Americans

31:17.450 --> 31:19.330
undertaking a patriotic duty.

31:19.830 --> 31:22.960
Your travail will earn you the love

31:23.210 --> 31:25.460
and gratitude of your country.

31:25.710 --> 31:27.800
I think it was Thomas Paine who said, "These are..."

31:27.880 --> 31:30.090
Lehman is a trading partner for every bank in this room.

31:30.180 --> 31:32.890
Their failure will be a massive blow to all of you.

31:32.970 --> 31:34.510
This is your problem.

31:34.680 --> 31:37.560
We can all argue about how we got here.

31:37.640 --> 31:41.230
Deregulation, derivatives, Dick Fuld made bad decisions.

31:41.600 --> 31:44.810
We're all responsible. It's a catastrophic mess.

31:45.730 --> 31:48.650
The government's done whatever it can do. It's on you now.

31:49.110 --> 31:51.490
You need to fix it and you need to pay for it.

31:51.740 --> 31:53.700
Let me be clear.

31:54.990 --> 31:59.500
We will remember anyone who is not helpful.

32:23.270 --> 32:25.100
Do you notice a little pattern here?

32:25.190 --> 32:27.940
We don't deal with things until there's some kind of emergency on the table.

32:28.030 --> 32:30.110
Yeah, we don't deal with things until Friday.

32:30.190 --> 32:31.320
Some kind of a weekend thing.

32:31.400 --> 32:32.450
When were we gonna figure out

32:32.530 --> 32:34.610
exactly how to handle the failure of an investment bank?

32:34.700 --> 32:36.320
Let's do it over a weekend.

32:36.410 --> 32:39.790
I talked to a person who is monitoring the situation out there,

32:39.870 --> 32:44.000
and the person said they all want the same deal Jamie Dimon got,

32:44.080 --> 32:46.130
and they're not going to get it.

32:46.290 --> 32:49.380
Just a heads up, Chris Flowers is in the lobby.

32:49.460 --> 32:52.380
He's consulting for B of A on Lehman.

32:53.720 --> 32:56.180
Hank pushed Flowers out of Goldman.

32:56.260 --> 32:59.890
Yesterday, we thought Lehman was holding 30 billion in crap. Today it's 70.

33:00.020 --> 33:03.390
$70 billion in assets that we're valuing with a dartboard.

33:03.480 --> 33:04.730
No, we're gonna work this out.

33:05.060 --> 33:07.310
Unless you step in, we're ending this.

33:07.400 --> 33:08.690
Don't forget about Dick.

33:08.770 --> 33:12.740
Yeah, Dick Fuld keeps calling, hammering us to raise the price.

33:14.860 --> 33:16.860
Excuse me.

33:20.490 --> 33:25.370
70 fucking billion and no collateral?

33:27.420 --> 33:29.590
If Lehman Brothers fails this weekend,

33:29.670 --> 33:33.920
there's little doubt amongst the people I speak to that Merrill Lynch will be next.

33:34.010 --> 33:36.180
Confidence is a very fragile thing.

33:36.260 --> 33:39.930
And in our financial markets, it is eroding very quickly.

33:40.010 --> 33:45.100
Lehman's not gonna make it. We're next. We need to do something right now.

33:45.480 --> 33:48.770
B of A's the only one out there. I can put in a call.

33:48.860 --> 33:50.940
- It screws Lehman, but...  - Lehman screwed themselves.

33:51.020 --> 33:56.530
Paulson has drawn a line in the sand. Lehman is dead. Make the call.

33:59.780 --> 34:03.120
We'd like you guys to look at taking a stake in Merrill,

34:03.200 --> 34:05.960
something in the neighborhood of 10%.

34:06.120 --> 34:09.790
I don't know if I can generate any interest in 10%,

34:10.630 --> 34:13.590
but if the whole company's in play,

34:13.670 --> 34:15.800
I bet that's something that we'd consider.

34:15.970 --> 34:17.930
Us and Lehman?

34:18.840 --> 34:20.720
Deal this size, there's only room for one.

34:27.560 --> 34:32.480
It is a lot of risk, no question, but if you stay in the game,

34:32.570 --> 34:34.980
- we all will find a private solution.  - Absolutely.

34:37.280 --> 34:41.660
Don't get scared by the numbers. We'll figure it out. Lehman is worth owning.

34:41.740 --> 34:44.450
Guys, you got a second?

34:48.040 --> 34:49.920
I was at AIG yesterday.

34:50.000 --> 34:54.590
Next Wednesday, they got a $5 billion hole, minimum.

34:55.760 --> 34:59.510
I called Warren Buffett. He wouldn't touch it with a stick.

35:00.680 --> 35:01.930
We're looking into it.

35:02.010 --> 35:03.970
It's a very big company.

35:06.350 --> 35:10.350
Thanks, Chris. I appreciate all your help.

35:17.530 --> 35:19.530
They're not on top of it.

35:26.290 --> 35:28.370
Look at this.

35:31.920 --> 35:34.420
Is this even possible?

35:34.920 --> 35:36.250
AIG?

35:36.550 --> 35:37.590
Holy shit.

35:39.550 --> 35:41.130
Get into this.

35:43.470 --> 35:44.510
John.

35:50.190 --> 35:52.770
Do you see what's happening here?

35:53.560 --> 35:55.440
Are you watching out for yourself?

35:55.520 --> 35:59.990
I'm not thick. I'm working on something.

36:03.620 --> 36:05.990
You're talking to B of A.

36:07.990 --> 36:09.330
I'm sorry.

36:10.370 --> 36:12.920
I know it's not good for Lehman, but I have to take care of Merrill.

36:13.080 --> 36:15.460
Let me worry about Lehman.

36:16.420 --> 36:19.130
Barclays is still in the game.

36:21.470 --> 36:22.590
Just get it done.

36:33.850 --> 36:37.360
B of A is gonna buy Merrill.

36:39.900 --> 36:41.690
We need to make Barclays work.

36:43.030 --> 36:45.320
I thought you said the British never close.

36:46.030 --> 36:49.910
They're here. They seem to really want it.

36:50.000 --> 36:53.210
If we hold their hands, they'll close.

36:53.290 --> 36:58.670
Rodgin, what the fuck? I can't get Geithner. I can't get Paulson.

36:58.750 --> 37:01.590
I can't even get my own goddamn employees to pick up the phone.

37:01.670 --> 37:03.630
There's a lot happening down here, Dick.

37:03.720 --> 37:05.130
Oh, fuck.

37:05.720 --> 37:07.930
You know what? You tell Geithner,

37:08.010 --> 37:11.060
he doesn't pull my ass out of the fire, Merrill's gonna be next.

37:11.180 --> 37:12.310
He knows that.

37:12.390 --> 37:16.770
Dick, he just told me they're working on a solution for Merrill.

37:20.190 --> 37:22.150
Those cocksuckers are gonna bail out Merrill?

37:22.240 --> 37:23.820
No, no, not the Fed.

37:24.110 --> 37:27.870
There's no private buyers. There's just B of A and...

37:29.450 --> 37:31.490
They're gonna put B of A together with Merrill?

37:31.580 --> 37:34.080
We still have Barclays. Hank feels that...

37:34.160 --> 37:37.960
The federal government is snaking my deal?

37:39.290 --> 37:41.800
I cannot fucking believe this shit!

37:44.470 --> 37:46.220
B of A made a deal with Merrill?

37:46.300 --> 37:48.300
Yeah, Lehman's still our problem.

37:49.010 --> 37:51.140
I don't think I can take another day of this.

37:51.220 --> 37:54.520
You're getting out of a Mercedes to go to the New York Federal Reserve.

37:54.600 --> 37:57.310
It's not a Higgins boat on Omaha Beach.

38:06.030 --> 38:08.780
Okay, okay, we've been here a long time.

38:09.870 --> 38:12.120
Let's stop fucking around.

38:12.200 --> 38:15.120
Main Street wants Wall Street to pay.

38:15.210 --> 38:18.040
They think we're overpaid assholes.

38:19.040 --> 38:21.590
There's no politician who's gonna sign off on a bailout.

38:21.670 --> 38:25.590
Why would you bail out people whose sole job is to make money?

38:25.670 --> 38:29.590
So, let's make this real simple.

38:30.600 --> 38:34.470
Hank thinks he can sell the good parts of Lehman to Barclays,

38:34.560 --> 38:38.890
but someone has to take the real estate crap that's weighing down the company.

38:38.980 --> 38:40.730
Nobody knows how much this shit is worth,

38:40.810 --> 38:42.940
but we know somebody has to buy it to make a deal.

38:43.400 --> 38:47.280
I don't like it. Nobody likes it.

38:47.820 --> 38:50.240
Let's just suck it up and do it.

38:50.620 --> 38:56.000
How many of you can kick in a billion dollars to stop Lehman from going down?

38:58.920 --> 39:01.500
I'm in. One billion.

39:14.010 --> 39:16.180
Okay.

39:21.480 --> 39:24.230
Goldman's in for a billion.

39:31.570 --> 39:32.990
John?

39:34.280 --> 39:35.410
Yeah?

39:41.420 --> 39:42.460
Yes.

39:45.050 --> 39:46.130
Yeah.

39:49.260 --> 39:50.300
Yes.

39:55.640 --> 39:57.890
We did it. Barclays came through.

39:58.140 --> 39:59.810
Too goddamn right!

39:59.890 --> 40:04.440
Barclays won't touch the real estate assets, but the other banks agreed to cover those.

40:06.480 --> 40:10.320
Call the board. Get everybody here. We got a deal to make.

40:10.530 --> 40:11.700
Okay.

40:21.920 --> 40:23.000
Did you get him?

40:23.080 --> 40:25.500
Geithner doesn't return my calls.

40:26.340 --> 40:27.960
We can't get hold of any of those people.

40:28.050 --> 40:30.920
At the end of the day, they can't sign the deal without our say-so.

40:31.010 --> 40:33.130
Yes, but do they know that?

40:36.760 --> 40:37.890
Yes.

40:38.810 --> 40:42.680
Yes, by all means, now that he deigns to call, put him through.

40:43.020 --> 40:45.690
Sir Callum, sorry I haven't gotten back to you.

40:45.770 --> 40:49.360
We're just sorting through this Barclays deal for Lehman.

40:50.360 --> 40:52.490
As Barclays' regulator,

40:52.570 --> 40:55.490
we have serious concerns about this proposed arrangement.

40:55.570 --> 40:59.450
What? Are you saying you won't approve the deal?

40:59.540 --> 41:03.000
Even if we were comfortable with the agreement, which we are not,

41:03.080 --> 41:07.080
Barclays would need at least 30 days for shareholder approval.

41:07.170 --> 41:11.880
No, no, no, no, no, no. He can't. He can't do this.

41:11.970 --> 41:14.630
- Can they stop the deal?  - Yeah, of course they can stop the deal.

41:14.720 --> 41:16.640
But even if they don't stop it, the delay will kill it.

41:16.720 --> 41:20.180
Let's call the Chancellor of the Exchequer right now.

41:20.390 --> 41:25.190
None of those assets would be part of Barclays.

41:26.310 --> 41:30.520
Yes, but if the FSA were to...

41:31.030 --> 41:32.280
We need stability.

41:32.360 --> 41:35.070
Of course.

41:35.160 --> 41:38.030
must be to the British financial system. Surely you see that.

41:38.120 --> 41:39.240
Yeah.

41:39.870 --> 41:42.240
I wish you good night and good luck.

41:43.410 --> 41:45.580
Good night to you, too.

41:53.300 --> 41:55.760
"We don't want to import your cancer."

42:08.350 --> 42:09.770
Those of you

42:11.230 --> 42:14.360
who didn't want to assist the Barclays deal,

42:14.440 --> 42:17.110
you can breathe a sigh of relief.

42:18.320 --> 42:19.410
The deal's not gonna happen.

42:19.490 --> 42:21.740
- Hey, we have the money.  - Deal's dead.

42:22.740 --> 42:25.120
British regulator won't sign off.

42:25.210 --> 42:26.790
There must be some pressure you can exert.

42:26.870 --> 42:29.460
Trust me, I've done everything I can.

42:29.790 --> 42:31.210
The British,

42:33.300 --> 42:35.260
they grin-fucked us.

42:38.550 --> 42:42.220
Paulson's got 100,000 employees. Geithner has 200 lawyers.

42:42.310 --> 42:44.720
Frigging Chris Cox has 3,000 people.

42:44.810 --> 42:47.520
Not a single one of those assholes could pick up a phone,

42:47.600 --> 42:50.100
call the British, find out if there was a deal to be made

42:50.190 --> 42:53.860
before they spend the weekend sweating us for cash?

42:53.940 --> 42:55.280
Somebody dropped the ball.

42:55.360 --> 42:57.190
They didn't drop the ball.

42:57.280 --> 42:59.070
They dropped the ball, kicked the coach in the nuts

42:59.160 --> 43:01.200
and took a crap in the quarterback's mouth.

43:01.280 --> 43:02.820
Of course.

43:05.790 --> 43:07.910
The Fed will do something.

43:09.120 --> 43:10.290
They have to.

43:15.380 --> 43:17.130
We've got to announce the Lehman bankruptcy now.

43:17.220 --> 43:20.840
If Asia opens and this isn't locked down, everyone's gonna assume the worst.

43:20.930 --> 43:23.140
The sell-off will kill the Merrill-B of A deal.

43:23.220 --> 43:25.600
- Who's gonna make the announcement?  - I think it's gotta be SEC.

43:25.680 --> 43:26.810
Yeah.

43:28.730 --> 43:29.850
Okay.

43:33.190 --> 43:35.320
Let's do it here. How soon can we set that up?

43:35.400 --> 43:36.730
Right away.

43:36.820 --> 43:39.530
We could use the main floor, the entrance hall.

43:39.610 --> 43:42.160
With the statue behind me? Excellent.

43:46.330 --> 43:48.870
Did Lehman actually agree to file?

43:57.760 --> 43:59.260
Chris, call Lehman.

43:59.340 --> 44:00.920
Make sure they file right now.

44:01.630 --> 44:02.680
Absolutely.

44:14.980 --> 44:16.860
AIG's a huge problem.

44:16.940 --> 44:19.650
The books are a mess. But from what I can tell,

44:19.740 --> 44:22.650
half the banks in the world are in bed with these guys.

44:22.740 --> 44:24.910
Everyone who had a piece-of-crap mortgage security

44:24.990 --> 44:28.410
bought insurance on their risk from AIG, and I mean everyone.

44:28.490 --> 44:30.830
How could they let this happen?

44:30.910 --> 44:34.960
They must have figured real estate would never go down, ever.

44:35.710 --> 44:37.630
Hank, Lehman still hasn't filed.

44:40.300 --> 44:43.930
Cox has to make his announcement. Asia's opening.

44:44.010 --> 44:45.180
Has he talked to them?

44:45.260 --> 44:47.470
He hasn't even called yet.

44:47.560 --> 44:50.140
We told Fuld about Barclays. We said Cox was gonna call.

44:50.220 --> 44:53.390
He's got his entire board sitting there, no call.

44:54.100 --> 44:55.230
Cox is like a deer in the headlights.

44:55.980 --> 44:57.940
Son of a bitch!

45:03.950 --> 45:07.740
You are their regulator. Call the Lehman board now.

45:07.830 --> 45:10.410
Hank, it's a complicated situation where...

45:10.500 --> 45:12.410
Asia's opening.

45:12.500 --> 45:14.250
They don't announce, the market goes in the toilet.

45:14.330 --> 45:17.380
We have to consider the appropriate role of government here.

45:17.460 --> 45:19.540
Can I, as Chairman of SEC, move forward and...

45:19.630 --> 45:22.050
You guys are like the gang that can't shoot straight.

45:22.130 --> 45:23.800
This is your job.

45:24.800 --> 45:27.970
Make the damn phone call.

45:35.270 --> 45:37.020
Chris, this is Dick Fuld.

45:37.110 --> 45:41.780
The board is in session. Everyone is here. All the directors and the firm's counsel.

45:41.860 --> 45:45.240
We understand the Barclays deal has imploded.

45:46.700 --> 45:52.410
We were hoping you might have some option that we haven't thought of yet.

45:53.040 --> 45:56.460
Um, the markets are in turmoil, as you know.

45:57.080 --> 45:59.210
A Lehman bankruptcy will help

45:59.290 --> 46:02.050
settle that uncertainty and, we believe, calm the market.

46:02.130 --> 46:04.880
Both the Fed and SEC are in agreement about this.

46:04.970 --> 46:08.800
Let me... I'm sorry, let me see if I understand this.

46:08.890 --> 46:12.680
Are you directing us to put Lehman into bankruptcy?

46:12.770 --> 46:14.560
Give us a moment.

46:15.980 --> 46:17.730
- He's muted.  - What the hell was that?

46:20.860 --> 46:22.900
Tell them to file.

46:22.980 --> 46:26.070
Everything said in that meeting is or will be public record.

46:26.150 --> 46:28.280
I can't tell a private company to file for bankruptcy.

46:28.360 --> 46:31.530
I don't have that power. Do I?

46:35.960 --> 46:37.040
Do it.

46:49.470 --> 46:55.060
The decision to file for bankruptcy protection is one the board needs to make.

46:56.020 --> 47:00.350
It is not the government's decision. But we do believe

47:02.730 --> 47:04.690
that the situation should be resolved.

47:04.860 --> 47:07.360
So you're not directing us to file?

47:07.900 --> 47:11.660
I'm not saying anything more than I've just said. Thank you.

47:24.840 --> 47:26.760
Take the vote.

47:28.420 --> 47:30.680
- Mr. Akers?  - Yes.

47:30.760 --> 47:32.340
Sir Christopher?

47:34.220 --> 47:35.350
Yes.

47:35.430 --> 47:37.350
Admiral Evans?

47:37.430 --> 47:40.140
I'm sorry, but yes.

47:40.940 --> 47:42.480
Chairman Fuld?

47:45.610 --> 47:46.940
Dick?

47:52.910 --> 47:54.030
Yes.

47:57.660 --> 48:00.750
- Mr. Hernandez?  - Yes.

48:02.330 --> 48:05.250
- Mr. Ainslie?  - Yes.

48:06.130 --> 48:08.420
- Mr. Berlind?  - Yes.

48:08.920 --> 48:11.550
- Mr. Cruikshank?  - Yes.

48:11.630 --> 48:14.890
- Dr. Kaufman?  - Yes.

48:14.970 --> 48:16.640
- Mr. Macomber?  - Yes.

48:19.770 --> 48:20.980
P?

48:21.890 --> 48:24.480
- Lehman's gone.  - Oh, honey.

48:24.560 --> 48:26.230
I should have known the British were gonna be a problem.

48:26.320 --> 48:28.860
I spoke to them two days ago. I should have read between the lines.

48:32.240 --> 48:37.410
You can't take all this on. There's a lot of other people involved.

48:37.490 --> 48:40.410
It's on me. I'm Treasury Secretary

48:40.500 --> 48:42.960
and the fourth-largest investment bank in the country

48:43.040 --> 48:44.790
failed on my watch.

48:45.750 --> 48:47.380
I mean...

48:50.170 --> 48:52.010
I don't know what's gonna happen.

48:54.510 --> 48:56.260
Miss you, too.

49:42.850 --> 49:44.230
Yo, taxi!

49:58.200 --> 50:00.240
Jim, I'm in midtown.

50:01.080 --> 50:02.910
The ticker is...

50:04.330 --> 50:05.960
What the hell am I looking at?

50:06.040 --> 50:08.380
Hank, it's good.

50:13.050 --> 50:15.220
"It is oddly reassuring

50:15.300 --> 50:18.010
"that the Treasury Department and the Federal Reserve

50:18.090 --> 50:19.510
let Lehman Brothers fail."

50:19.600 --> 50:21.640
Okay, here it is. Here it is.

50:21.720 --> 50:24.020
"Government intervention would have been seen

50:24.100 --> 50:27.770
"as a sign of extreme peril in the global financial system

50:27.850 --> 50:30.690
"or extreme weakness on the part of federal regulators."

50:30.770 --> 50:33.070
The New York fucking Times.

50:33.150 --> 50:36.150
Yeah, and it's not just them. It's everybody.

50:36.240 --> 50:38.610
Thank you, Senator. I appreciate it.

50:39.990 --> 50:42.370
Congress is liking it.

50:42.450 --> 50:44.200
I'm not sure they should be so happy about this,

50:44.290 --> 50:47.370
but they like that we let Lehman go.

50:47.460 --> 50:52.670
You stood strong. You've got brass balls. It's what people need at a time like this.

50:53.460 --> 50:54.750
What is the market doing?

50:54.840 --> 50:58.340
Europe's only down a little. Dow Jones futures only off 3 %.

50:58.430 --> 51:00.430
So you need to build on this at the press conference.

51:00.510 --> 51:03.810
The story is we did everything we could for Lehman.

51:03.890 --> 51:05.560
- We did.  - And the takeaway is...

51:05.640 --> 51:07.270
No more bailouts.

51:08.600 --> 51:10.020
Mr. Secretary.

51:10.100 --> 51:11.810
How should we read what happened with Lehman?

51:12.020 --> 51:16.570
Read it as I think it's important to maintain the stability

51:16.650 --> 51:20.240
and orderliness of our financial system,

51:20.320 --> 51:23.780
and, um, the

51:26.120 --> 51:28.580
moral hazard is something I don't take lightly.

51:28.660 --> 51:30.830
- Mr. Paulson.  - Mr. Secretary, sir.

51:30.920 --> 51:34.420
How do you see the health of the banking system today?

51:34.500 --> 51:36.090
The way the markets are performing today,

51:36.170 --> 51:38.590
it's a testament to how the financial industry has come together

51:38.670 --> 51:41.470
in an extraordinary set of circumstances.

51:41.550 --> 51:45.760
The American people can remain confident in the soundness and the resilience

51:45.850 --> 51:49.430
of the financial system. Thank you all.

51:49.520 --> 51:52.520
- Mr. Secretary.  - Mr. Paulson.

51:53.150 --> 51:54.190
What?

51:54.270 --> 51:57.320
Dow is falling off a cliff. Four hundred points already.

51:57.400 --> 51:59.440
And the credit markets are frozen.

51:59.530 --> 52:02.110
Goldman and Morgan Stanley are getting slammed by withdrawals.

52:02.200 --> 52:04.280
The Lehman thing's killing them.

52:06.040 --> 52:10.210
I should talk to the President about how we'll contain the damage.

52:10.370 --> 52:12.830
Before you speak to the President,

52:13.540 --> 52:14.790
we've got another problem.

52:16.630 --> 52:17.960
AIG.

52:18.300 --> 52:22.010
Mr. Geithner is working with AIG on a private-sector solution

52:22.090 --> 52:26.760
and the Secretary is monitoring their progress very carefully.

52:26.850 --> 52:30.850
He's in close contact with the Congressional leadership, most...

52:30.940 --> 52:32.810
White House wants a briefing on AIG.

52:32.900 --> 52:36.270
You're more informed about that than we are. We've got the same information you do.

52:36.400 --> 52:39.320
No, we don't believe that Lehman's bankruptcy

52:39.400 --> 52:41.440
is taking down all of Wall Street.

52:41.530 --> 52:42.820
Okay, but how do you explain...

52:42.910 --> 52:47.910
The AIG situation may have been, you know, exacerbated by the...

52:47.990 --> 52:49.740
And Barney Frank's office.

52:51.750 --> 52:53.620
- Yeah.  - A surprise?

52:54.540 --> 52:56.040
No, AIG has been struggling...

52:56.130 --> 52:59.130
Why didn't you guys head off this situation six months ago?

52:59.510 --> 53:03.090
Because the requisite magic wand wasn't available, Doug.

53:03.340 --> 53:06.720
But if we do find one, you will be among the very first to know.

53:08.430 --> 53:10.430
Blankfein on four, urgent.

53:10.520 --> 53:11.930
I have to go.

53:13.190 --> 53:14.230
Hey, Lloyd.

53:14.310 --> 53:16.690
The goddamn British bankruptcy administrator

53:16.770 --> 53:19.110
froze all of Lehman's customer accounts.

53:19.190 --> 53:21.530
You didn't square this with the British before you did it?

53:21.610 --> 53:23.070
Geithner on five.

53:23.150 --> 53:26.700
People are freaking out. They can't get their money.

53:27.490 --> 53:29.530
Not investors, clients.

53:29.620 --> 53:32.160
If customers think their money wasn't safe with Lehman,

53:32.250 --> 53:33.790
they're not gonna trust us either.

53:33.870 --> 53:37.000
No one will do business with us.

53:37.080 --> 53:39.000
You're gonna have to hang on.

53:39.090 --> 53:40.130
Tim.

53:40.340 --> 53:42.880
Dan spoke to Moody's. They're downgrading AIG tonight.

53:42.970 --> 53:46.090
Would have happened anyway, but Lehman put the whole thing into hyperdrive.

53:46.180 --> 53:47.720
How far down are they?

53:47.800 --> 53:49.300
French Finance Minister on six.

53:49.390 --> 53:51.010
They don't know.

53:52.140 --> 53:53.930
We're working on it.

53:54.980 --> 53:56.190
Christine.

53:56.270 --> 53:58.060
How could you let this happen with Lehman?

53:58.480 --> 53:59.730
What on earth were you thinking?

53:59.820 --> 54:02.530
We did everything we could, Christine.

54:02.610 --> 54:06.700
You allowed it to fail, Hank. It was a horrifying mistake.

54:06.780 --> 54:09.280
It is having catastrophic consequences all over Europe.

54:09.370 --> 54:11.910
And AIG would be even worse.

54:11.990 --> 54:15.620
The European banks have tremendous exposure to AIG.

54:15.830 --> 54:18.830
We need assurance you will not fail to act, as you did with Lehman.

54:18.920 --> 54:21.630
It is not just an American problem.

54:22.840 --> 54:24.010
Thank you so much for...

54:24.340 --> 54:25.380
John Mack.

54:27.180 --> 54:28.840
- John.  - John, one.

54:30.800 --> 54:33.430
The fallout from Lehman is killing us.

54:33.520 --> 54:37.350
The short-sellers are all over us. We're down 10 % already.

54:37.440 --> 54:39.730
You guys have no idea what's going on out here.

54:40.980 --> 54:42.270
How are your earnings?

54:42.360 --> 54:44.780
Strong. We're gonna announce Friday.

54:44.860 --> 54:45.900
Announce now.

54:47.950 --> 54:49.860
Okay, Lloyd, sorry.

54:50.200 --> 54:53.120
Hank, if depositors see they can't get their assets out of Lehman,

54:53.200 --> 54:57.080
the next step is gonna be a massive pullout from all the investment banks.

54:57.160 --> 54:58.580
Jeff Immelt from GE.

55:00.170 --> 55:02.460
Jesus, what's Immelt calling you for?

55:02.540 --> 55:05.800
I have to go. Jeff.

55:05.880 --> 55:07.590
You gotta know what's going on out here.

55:08.050 --> 55:11.220
We're having trouble funding our day-to-day operations.

55:11.680 --> 55:14.970
Our finance division is infecting the rest of our business.

55:15.060 --> 55:16.560
General Electric?

55:16.640 --> 55:19.980
This thing is spreading way past Wall Street.

55:20.060 --> 55:22.190
I get that nobody wants to touch mortgages,

55:22.270 --> 55:25.820
but we're making planes and engines, light bulbs.

55:26.490 --> 55:28.190
We're a healthy company.

55:28.280 --> 55:30.990
If we can't finance our day-to-day operations,

55:31.070 --> 55:34.330
every business in America's gonna be shutting down.

55:43.380 --> 55:45.630
- Where's AIG?  - It closed at $2.

55:45.710 --> 55:49.510
Tim thinks we need to do something big. Ben agrees, and so do I.

55:49.590 --> 55:51.510
What can we do? AIG's not even a bank.

55:51.590 --> 55:56.510
The Fed can lend to non-banks under unusual and exigent circumstances.

55:56.600 --> 55:59.850
We're thinking of taking over 80% of the company.

56:02.600 --> 56:05.110
Hank, we can't.

56:05.190 --> 56:07.900
This morning we were lecturing the entire country on moral hazard.

56:07.990 --> 56:10.860
AIG has collateral. They have assets.

56:10.950 --> 56:14.410
Lehman didn't. We couldn't lend into a hole.

56:14.490 --> 56:15.950
It's not the same story.

56:16.040 --> 56:17.830
Nobody's gonna care.

56:17.910 --> 56:21.040
It's another bailout with no legislation.

56:21.120 --> 56:23.000
The Hill's gonna go crazy. The country's gonna go crazy.

56:23.080 --> 56:26.840
The plane that we flew in on this morning, leased from AIG.

56:26.920 --> 56:29.090
Construction downtown, AIG.

56:29.840 --> 56:35.090
Life insurance, 81 million policies with a face value of 1.9 trillion,

56:35.180 --> 56:37.890
billions of dollars in teachers' pensions, it's everywhere.

56:37.970 --> 56:40.520
You want too big to fail? Here it is.

56:41.100 --> 56:44.440
You got a better idea? The suggestion box is wide open.

56:51.990 --> 56:53.950
I hate to do this right now,

56:54.030 --> 56:56.320
but I'm gonna have to have a press call first thing,

56:56.410 --> 56:58.330
and I really don't know what I'm gonna tell them.

56:58.790 --> 57:03.830
Tell them Lehman exacerbated AIG. The simultaneous payouts of CDOs

57:03.920 --> 57:06.380
and credit default swaps put catastrophic pressure on...

57:06.460 --> 57:08.040
Go back further.

57:09.250 --> 57:12.130
The global pool of investment capital...

57:12.220 --> 57:15.930
She has to do this in English. Start with the homeowners.

57:16.010 --> 57:18.680
Okay, okay, here's how you explain it.

57:20.520 --> 57:25.900
Wall Street started bundling home loans together, mortgage-backed securities,

57:26.190 --> 57:29.400
and selling slices of those bundles to investors.

57:29.480 --> 57:31.440
And they were making big money,

57:31.530 --> 57:34.110
so they started pushing the lenders, saying,

57:34.200 --> 57:35.780
"Come on, we need more loans."

57:35.990 --> 57:39.410
The lenders had already given loans to borrowers with good credit,

57:39.490 --> 57:42.370
so they go bottom-feeding. They lower their criteria.

57:42.450 --> 57:47.170
Before, you needed a credit score of 620 and a down payment of 20%.

57:47.380 --> 57:49.960
Now, they'll settle for 500, no money down.

57:50.050 --> 57:52.050
And the buyer, the regular guy on the street,

57:52.130 --> 57:53.920
assumes that the experts know what they're doing.

57:54.010 --> 57:56.590
He's saying to himself, "If the bank's willing to loan me money,

57:56.680 --> 57:58.430
"I must be able to afford it."

57:58.680 --> 58:00.560
So he reaches for the American dream.

58:00.640 --> 58:01.970
He buys that house.

58:02.060 --> 58:05.480
The banks knew securities based on shitbag mortgages were risky.

58:05.560 --> 58:06.810
You'll work on "shitbag."

58:06.900 --> 58:10.400
So to control their downside, the banks started buying a kind of insurance.

58:10.480 --> 58:13.440
If mortgages default, insurance company pays,

58:13.690 --> 58:15.240
default swap.

58:15.320 --> 58:19.530
The banks insure their potential losses to move the risk off their books

58:19.620 --> 58:21.620
so they can invest more, make more money.

58:21.700 --> 58:23.830
And while a lot of companies insured this stuff,

58:23.910 --> 58:27.370
one was dumb enough to take on an almost unbelievable amount of risk.

58:27.540 --> 58:29.790
- AIG.  - And you'll work on "dumb."

58:29.880 --> 58:32.040
And when they ask me why they did that?

58:32.340 --> 58:34.380
- Fees.  - Hundreds of millions in fees.

58:34.460 --> 58:37.010
AIG figured the housing market would just keep going up,

58:37.090 --> 58:38.430
but then the unexpected happens.

58:38.510 --> 58:40.680
Housing prices go down.

58:40.760 --> 58:42.560
The poor bastard who bought his dream house,

58:42.640 --> 58:44.720
the teaser rate on his mortgage runs out.

58:44.810 --> 58:47.190
His payments go up. He defaults.

58:47.270 --> 58:51.520
Mortgage-backed securities tank. AIG has to pay off the swaps,

58:51.610 --> 58:55.280
all of them, all over the world, at the same time.

58:55.440 --> 58:58.320
AIG can't pay. AIG goes under.

58:59.320 --> 59:03.580
Every bank they insure books massive losses on the same day.

59:04.950 --> 59:06.750
And then they all go under.

59:06.960 --> 59:08.660
It all comes down.

59:10.670 --> 59:12.500
The whole financial system?

59:18.050 --> 59:22.430
And what do I say when they ask me why it wasn't regulated?

59:23.310 --> 59:25.060
No one wanted to.

59:26.930 --> 59:29.310
We were making too much money.

59:39.740 --> 59:43.990
You'll work on "We were making too much money."

59:56.840 --> 59:59.340
Hey, Hank. Um...

01:00:02.090 --> 01:00:04.850
These are really mild, but they work.

01:00:06.010 --> 01:00:10.850
I know the whole Christian Scientist thing, that you don't take medicine.

01:00:12.480 --> 01:00:14.440
But you need to sleep.

01:00:17.070 --> 01:00:21.860
- Jim...  - Hank, not just for your own sake. Okay?

01:00:27.540 --> 01:00:28.830
You don't look so good.

01:00:33.580 --> 01:00:38.380
Just minutes ago, CNN has confirmed that the Federal Reserve is negotiating

01:00:38.460 --> 01:00:40.970
to rescue insurance giant AIG.

01:00:41.050 --> 01:00:45.340
The negotiations could include an $85 billion temporary loan

01:00:45.430 --> 01:00:47.640
to stave off AIG's imminent collapse.

01:00:47.720 --> 01:00:49.140
This is a blockbuster, folks.

01:00:49.220 --> 01:00:51.520
About this time last night, many on Wall Street

01:00:51.600 --> 01:00:53.770
were fearing total disaster today.

01:00:53.850 --> 01:00:57.570
So, another federal bailout of another Wall Street giant.

01:00:57.650 --> 01:01:00.440
Again, as we mentioned, big, big financial news.

01:01:00.530 --> 01:01:03.860
And this affects almost everyone out there who has a 401k.

01:01:03.950 --> 01:01:08.330
The news comes about three and a half hours after the stock exchange closed on...

01:01:08.410 --> 01:01:10.080
Motherfucker.

01:01:10.950 --> 01:01:15.290
We wanna go straight right now to Ali Velshi to kind of give us a bottom line on this, Ali.

01:01:15.380 --> 01:01:16.540
And then we're gonna get a lot more in depth...

01:01:16.630 --> 01:01:18.960
AIG securing an 85...

01:01:19.050 --> 01:01:20.960
The credit markets are still frozen solid.

01:01:21.050 --> 01:01:23.470
We keep dumping money into these companies

01:01:23.550 --> 01:01:25.760
so the markets will stabilize,

01:01:26.550 --> 01:01:27.640
and nothing helps.

01:01:27.720 --> 01:01:29.810
We can't keep doing this.

01:01:30.770 --> 01:01:34.940
We rescued Bear with no legislation, AIG with no legislation.

01:01:36.520 --> 01:01:38.770
People think Fannie and Freddie was a bait-and-switch.

01:01:38.860 --> 01:01:42.360
I don't like it any more than you do, but they were exceptional situations.

01:01:42.440 --> 01:01:45.450
This situation demands an endgame

01:01:45.530 --> 01:01:48.910
that does not rely on unilateral action from the Fed.

01:01:48.990 --> 01:01:50.330
This is a democracy.

01:01:50.410 --> 01:01:52.950
We cannot be men behind the curtain pulling the strings.

01:01:53.290 --> 01:01:57.670
We need some kind of an overall solution that heals the entire system.

01:01:57.750 --> 01:01:59.710
And it has to be legitimate.

01:01:59.800 --> 01:02:01.340
We've been putting off going to Congress.

01:02:01.420 --> 01:02:03.090
It's two months before an election.

01:02:03.170 --> 01:02:08.890
They can't vote yes to a massive bailout of the entire banking system.

01:02:08.970 --> 01:02:10.430
- They'd never get reelected.  - Hank...

01:02:10.640 --> 01:02:14.430
We go to Congress, publically state we don't have the firepower

01:02:14.520 --> 01:02:18.560
to control this anymore, and then they say no,

01:02:19.520 --> 01:02:22.980
that is really the nightmare scenario.

01:02:23.530 --> 01:02:26.820
The nightmare scenario's already here, my friend.

01:02:27.740 --> 01:02:29.240
We're in it.

01:03:30.680 --> 01:03:32.050
P.

01:03:43.690 --> 01:03:45.730
You can't put this all on your own shoulders.

01:03:45.820 --> 01:03:47.150
We're running out of options.

01:03:48.740 --> 01:03:51.990
Then you just keep trying. That's what you do.

01:03:52.070 --> 01:03:54.070
Eventually something's gonna work.

01:03:54.160 --> 01:03:56.910
I'm not sure that that is true anymore.

01:03:58.000 --> 01:04:01.580
There's not a bank in the world that has enough money in its vault

01:04:01.670 --> 01:04:05.750
to pay its depositors. It's all built on trust.

01:04:05.840 --> 01:04:11.430
And, Wendy, we are so very close.

01:04:14.720 --> 01:04:18.390
Morgan Stanley, Goldman are an inch away.

01:04:18.480 --> 01:04:24.020
If the other banks stop trusting them, if they pull back on interbank lending,

01:04:24.110 --> 01:04:26.440
it's over in a matter of hours.

01:04:27.110 --> 01:04:31.200
And from there it goes too fast to stop, a run,

01:04:31.280 --> 01:04:36.240
and not just on one bank, I mean on the whole system.

01:04:36.330 --> 01:04:40.370
And average people wondering, "Is my money safe?"

01:04:41.620 --> 01:04:43.960
They start pulling their cash.

01:04:46.130 --> 01:04:50.630
And after that, lines outside the banks, smashed ATMs.

01:04:51.630 --> 01:04:53.470
A couple of weeks,

01:04:54.970 --> 01:04:57.350
there's no milk in the store.

01:04:58.310 --> 01:04:59.520
For the second time this week,

01:04:59.600 --> 01:05:01.560
there was a massive sell-off on Wall Street,

01:05:01.640 --> 01:05:05.020
the Dow Jones Industrial's freefalling some 450 points

01:05:05.110 --> 01:05:09.320
on news of a taxpayer-funded bailout of the AIG insurance company.

01:05:09.400 --> 01:05:11.490
Three of the five investment banks are gone.

01:05:11.570 --> 01:05:13.900
The country's biggest mortgage lender is gone.

01:05:13.990 --> 01:05:16.700
Fannie Mae, Freddie Mac, brought under government control.

01:05:16.780 --> 01:05:20.490
The financial system is holding right now, but for how long?

01:05:20.830 --> 01:05:22.960
All the king's horses and all the king's men

01:05:23.040 --> 01:05:25.080
are being brought to bear to keep this thing together.

01:05:52.240 --> 01:05:54.530
- Hi, Tim.  - I'm on the Street, Hank,

01:05:54.610 --> 01:05:57.280
and people are just going about their business.

01:05:57.370 --> 01:06:01.080
They have no idea the whole thing is about to fall down.

01:06:02.000 --> 01:06:05.620
We're gonna have to go to Congress. We don't have a choice anymore.

01:06:05.710 --> 01:06:06.830
Okay.

01:06:06.920 --> 01:06:08.460
How fast do you think you can get them to move?

01:06:08.540 --> 01:06:10.710
It's gonna be at least a couple days.

01:06:11.300 --> 01:06:14.170
Goldman and Morgan Stanley are going down now.

01:06:14.260 --> 01:06:15.630
We gotta do something today.

01:06:15.720 --> 01:06:16.840
Like what?

01:06:16.930 --> 01:06:19.470
The market doesn't like investment banks, right?

01:06:19.550 --> 01:06:21.760
The money's saying "fuck you" to the whole business model.

01:06:21.850 --> 01:06:22.890
Yeah.

01:06:22.970 --> 01:06:26.060
Yeah, so merge them with commercial banks,

01:06:26.140 --> 01:06:28.650
turn them into regular banks regulated by the Fed.

01:06:28.730 --> 01:06:30.150
They can use depositors' cash.

01:06:30.230 --> 01:06:31.860
It will give them access to the discount window.

01:06:31.940 --> 01:06:34.990
It's cheap government cash.

01:06:35.070 --> 01:06:37.820
- You wanna make them bigger?  - It's a trade-off, Hank.

01:06:37.910 --> 01:06:40.950
The upside is it'll stabilize them.

01:06:42.370 --> 01:06:44.580
The downside is,

01:06:45.580 --> 01:06:48.330
yeah, they'll be really fucking big.

01:06:48.420 --> 01:06:50.000
Do it, Tim.

01:06:52.960 --> 01:06:54.250
Godspeed.

01:07:12.020 --> 01:07:14.190
Uh, get me Blankfein.

01:07:16.110 --> 01:07:18.110
Lloyd, call Vikram Pandit, talk to him about a merger.

01:07:18.200 --> 01:07:20.240
Tim, we're looking at all our options.

01:07:20.370 --> 01:07:23.870
You don't have options, Lloyd. There's a run on your bank.

01:07:23.950 --> 01:07:25.580
You need money. Citi has deposits.

01:07:25.660 --> 01:07:28.330
Citi's a mess, you're organized. Call Pandit.

01:07:37.760 --> 01:07:38.800
Yeah.

01:07:38.880 --> 01:07:40.840
I want you to look at merging with Morgan Stanley.

01:07:40.930 --> 01:07:43.050
What, are you kidding me? I just bought Bear.

01:07:43.140 --> 01:07:44.510
We're still trying to make that work.

01:07:44.600 --> 01:07:46.390
You didn't buy Bear. You got Bear as a Christmas present.

01:07:46.470 --> 01:07:50.640
The market's a fucking disaster, Jamie. Talk to John Mack.

01:07:50.730 --> 01:07:53.610
Hey, Geithner says we should talk about a merger.

01:07:53.690 --> 01:07:55.520
- You wanna do a deal?  - No.

01:07:55.610 --> 01:07:57.690
Then why the fuck did he tell me to call you?

01:07:57.780 --> 01:07:59.860
I'm under orders to try and help you.

01:07:59.990 --> 01:08:03.070
You're a prince, Jamie. A real prince.

01:08:07.000 --> 01:08:11.500
Apparently, some people seem to think combining our firms would be a good idea.

01:08:11.710 --> 01:08:14.380
I want you to know I'm flattered by this call.

01:08:14.920 --> 01:08:18.670
Vikram, I'm not trying to flatter you, believe me.

01:08:18.760 --> 01:08:20.260
Geithner's calling.

01:08:20.340 --> 01:08:22.930
Here comes goddamn eHarmony.

01:08:23.010 --> 01:08:24.970
Hey, guess what. Jamie's not interested.

01:08:28.140 --> 01:08:29.270
Do Wachovia.

01:08:29.350 --> 01:08:31.310
They were writing mortgages with their eyes closed.

01:08:31.390 --> 01:08:33.440
Tim, it's a shit sandwich. Forget it.

01:08:39.360 --> 01:08:41.280
I haven't been able to reach you for four hours.

01:08:41.360 --> 01:08:44.780
That is totally fucking unacceptable on a day like today.

01:08:45.030 --> 01:08:48.080
I had to run my team through the Goldman proposal.

01:08:48.160 --> 01:08:49.450
It's not gonna work.

01:08:50.330 --> 01:08:52.870
You passed on Goldman without even talking to me?

01:08:53.040 --> 01:08:55.880
I don't need another trillion dollars on my balance sheet.

01:08:59.670 --> 01:09:03.720
Next time I call, pick up.

01:09:05.350 --> 01:09:06.850
Fucker.

01:09:07.100 --> 01:09:10.470
The problem is the toxic assets. Let's just buy them.

01:09:10.560 --> 01:09:12.390
Ooh. We'll call it Cash for Trash.

01:09:12.560 --> 01:09:15.060
When the market stabilizes, we'll unload them.

01:09:15.150 --> 01:09:17.190
Hopefully, Treasury will get its money back.

01:09:17.270 --> 01:09:19.730
- Is that in the Break the Glass plan?  - Basically.

01:09:19.820 --> 01:09:22.740
My copy's missing some pages. Could someone else show me the rest of...

01:09:22.820 --> 01:09:24.490
It's succinct.

01:09:25.200 --> 01:09:27.660
We've got one shot with Congress.

01:09:27.740 --> 01:09:31.290
You want them to write a piece of legislation based on three pages?

01:09:31.370 --> 01:09:32.410
Look, guys,

01:09:32.790 --> 01:09:35.120
buying toxic assets is gonna be a nightmare.

01:09:35.210 --> 01:09:36.500
Nobody knows what the stuff's worth.

01:09:36.580 --> 01:09:39.340
It'll take forever to untangle. These banks are going down now.

01:09:39.420 --> 01:09:40.500
So what do we do?

01:09:40.590 --> 01:09:43.920
You wanna unfreeze credit, right? You want the banks to lend people money?

01:09:44.010 --> 01:09:46.680
- That's the goal.  - So give them money to lend.

01:09:49.810 --> 01:09:51.140
Capital injections?

01:09:51.220 --> 01:09:52.270
Dan, come on.

01:09:52.350 --> 01:09:53.850
I can't believe you're pulling out the "N" word.

01:09:53.940 --> 01:09:55.140
I'm not saying nationalize the banks.

01:09:55.230 --> 01:09:58.560
I'm saying isolate the ones that are really in trouble and make an investment.

01:09:58.650 --> 01:10:01.860
- It's un-American.  - It's sure un-Republican.

01:10:01.940 --> 01:10:05.200
You'd make the government a stockholder.

01:10:05.280 --> 01:10:08.990
What, do we tell them how to manage themselves, what to buy and sell?

01:10:09.080 --> 01:10:11.950
Run them like the post office? 'Cause that works like a dream.

01:10:12.040 --> 01:10:15.960
We make it temporary. We make ourselves non-voting stockholders.

01:10:16.170 --> 01:10:19.380
You can't just hand the banks massive piles of cash.

01:10:19.460 --> 01:10:21.040
Nobody's gonna go for it.

01:10:21.130 --> 01:10:25.010
To the Republicans it's nationalization. To the Democrats it's a bailout.

01:10:25.090 --> 01:10:26.680
And the banks are gonna go ballistic.

01:10:26.840 --> 01:10:31.810
Sorry, Dan, not gonna happen. Kudos for out-of-the-box thinking.

01:10:31.890 --> 01:10:35.310
Neel, if we go with your plan to buy the assets,

01:10:35.390 --> 01:10:36.560
how much is it gonna cost?

01:10:36.640 --> 01:10:38.020
It won't work.

01:10:38.100 --> 01:10:41.610
Look, the plan is three pages long, totally vague.

01:10:41.690 --> 01:10:46.070
If buying the assets doesn't work, we'll nationalize the banks for you, okay?

01:10:46.320 --> 01:10:50.120
- How much?  - To be safe, maybe a trillion.

01:10:50.830 --> 01:10:53.370
The Congress will never do a trillion, never.

01:10:53.540 --> 01:10:57.040
There's $11 trillion in residential mortgages.

01:10:57.120 --> 01:11:02.420
There's three trillion in commercial mortgages. That's 14 trillion, right?

01:11:02.500 --> 01:11:05.550
You gotta figure about 5% default rate.

01:11:06.510 --> 01:11:09.130
5 % of 14 trillion is

01:11:10.260 --> 01:11:12.220
$700 billion.

01:11:15.520 --> 01:11:17.770
It's better than a trillion.

01:11:21.440 --> 01:11:24.730
The only way to get this done is to just scare them shitless.

01:11:24.820 --> 01:11:28.030
That should be easy. I'm scared shitless myself.

01:11:41.040 --> 01:11:43.380
Troubled Asset Relief Program?

01:11:43.460 --> 01:11:44.880
TARP, we're calling it.

01:11:44.960 --> 01:11:48.260
This is your draft of the bill?

01:11:48.340 --> 01:11:50.720
Subject to revision, certainly.

01:11:54.010 --> 01:11:56.010
I've only got three pages here. Am I missing something?

01:11:56.640 --> 01:11:58.430
No.

01:11:59.730 --> 01:12:02.600
You're just looking for a big blank check in a hurry.

01:12:02.900 --> 01:12:06.320
It's all for the banks. You don't want a penny

01:12:06.400 --> 01:12:08.610
for the average Joe that's about to lose his house?

01:12:08.700 --> 01:12:10.780
There's not a scrap of oversight built into this,

01:12:10.860 --> 01:12:13.620
not for the banks or for you, for that matter.

01:12:13.700 --> 01:12:18.450
You want us to simply hand over $700 billion and trust you?

01:12:18.540 --> 01:12:20.750
How is it possible you didn't see this coming?

01:12:21.370 --> 01:12:23.460
If you wanna rewind the tape, we can do that.

01:12:23.540 --> 01:12:26.800
But right now, the one thing we don't have is time.

01:12:27.420 --> 01:12:29.510
We need an announcement tonight to calm the market.

01:12:29.590 --> 01:12:31.220
We need legislation next week.

01:12:34.970 --> 01:12:38.770
I spent my entire academic career

01:12:39.770 --> 01:12:42.230
studying the Great Depression.

01:12:44.940 --> 01:12:48.940
The Depression may have started because of a stock market crash,

01:12:49.030 --> 01:12:53.700
but what hit the general economy was a disruption of credit,

01:12:54.120 --> 01:12:58.700
average citizens unable to borrow money to do anything, to buy a home,

01:12:58.790 --> 01:13:01.620
start a business, stock their shelves.

01:13:03.170 --> 01:13:06.330
Credit has the ability to build a modern economy,

01:13:06.460 --> 01:13:11.050
but lack of credit has the power to destroy it, swiftly and absolutely.

01:13:13.130 --> 01:13:19.060
If we do not act boldly and immediately, we will replay the Depression of the 1930s,

01:13:19.140 --> 01:13:23.980
only this time it will be far, far worse.

01:13:27.320 --> 01:13:31.900
We don't do this now, we won't have an economy on Monday.

01:13:40.330 --> 01:13:41.660
Geithner.

01:13:46.670 --> 01:13:48.500
We took the proposal down to Congress.

01:13:48.590 --> 01:13:49.750
Are they gonna go for it?

01:13:49.840 --> 01:13:51.550
They didn't like the fact that it was short.

01:13:52.050 --> 01:13:53.300
I thought concise was a plus.

01:13:53.380 --> 01:13:56.760
They thought it was flimsy or arrogant or something.

01:13:56.840 --> 01:13:59.680
I don't know what they think. They couldn't get past it.

01:13:59.760 --> 01:14:02.100
The market's in a death spiral. They're gonna sink the bill

01:14:02.180 --> 01:14:03.930
because the proposal is too fucking short?

01:14:04.020 --> 01:14:07.560
They're working on it. They're gonna make it longer,

01:14:07.650 --> 01:14:09.520
among other things.

01:14:10.610 --> 01:14:13.940
Look, just tell me you got something.

01:14:16.990 --> 01:14:19.950
No. No, we're nowhere.

01:14:20.030 --> 01:14:21.540
Who's left for Morgan Stanley?

01:14:22.370 --> 01:14:25.790
China Investment Corporation.

01:14:27.540 --> 01:14:29.040
Uh, maybe Mitsubishi.

01:14:29.130 --> 01:14:31.710
No, the Japanese will never do this deal.

01:14:32.170 --> 01:14:33.300
How about Goldman?

01:14:33.460 --> 01:14:36.130
Goldman's looking at Wachovia, but they're so fucked,

01:14:36.220 --> 01:14:38.640
even John Mack turned up his nose.

01:14:41.430 --> 01:14:42.930
You gotta get that bill passed.

01:14:43.020 --> 01:14:47.560
- I'm pushing.  - Well, push harder. Please.

01:14:50.650 --> 01:14:53.280
This isn't working, Hank.

01:15:07.670 --> 01:15:09.250
Hank?

01:15:09.380 --> 01:15:11.500
Could you excuse me a moment?

01:15:33.150 --> 01:15:37.860
Oh, my God. Gao, Gao.

01:15:37.950 --> 01:15:41.410
I am so sorry about this. There's nothing we can do.

01:15:41.490 --> 01:15:45.330
It's New York, you know. We're lucky we can still buy a beer at Yankee Stadium.

01:15:45.410 --> 01:15:48.160
Don't worry about it. I should quit anyway.

01:15:48.580 --> 01:15:50.960
When you're ready, we'll be inside.

01:15:54.380 --> 01:15:56.050
Is there any movement there?

01:15:56.130 --> 01:16:00.630
They made an extremely low opening offer. I pretended I didn't hear it.

01:16:00.800 --> 01:16:04.970
The markets can't open Monday without a resolution of Morgan Stanley.

01:16:05.140 --> 01:16:07.470
We really need you to do a deal.

01:16:07.640 --> 01:16:09.890
- You need to call Jamie.  - I called Jamie.

01:16:09.980 --> 01:16:12.150
He doesn't want us. There's too much overlap.

01:16:12.230 --> 01:16:13.560
We've spoken to him. He'll do it.

01:16:13.770 --> 01:16:16.520
For what, a dollar? Why would I do that?

01:16:16.610 --> 01:16:19.650
I got China Investment and Mitsubishi kicking my tires.

01:16:19.860 --> 01:16:23.200
I know those guys. They're not gonna close a deal in time.

01:16:23.410 --> 01:16:26.410
We want you to call Jamie.

01:16:26.490 --> 01:16:28.620
Let me ask you a question.

01:16:28.700 --> 01:16:33.500
35,000 jobs just disappeared in this city between AIG, Lehman and Bear.

01:16:33.580 --> 01:16:38.170
We make a deal with Jamie, he'll fire 20,000 Morgan Stanley employees.

01:16:38.260 --> 01:16:40.170
You think that's good public policy?

01:16:40.340 --> 01:16:43.680
John, we are looking at the whole system here.

01:16:43.840 --> 01:16:45.350
The Chinese are leaving.

01:16:45.430 --> 01:16:47.180
Gao found out we're also talking to Mitsubishi.

01:16:47.270 --> 01:16:48.930
I gotta go.

01:16:49.020 --> 01:16:52.600
All right, get me Kuroyanagi and a Japanese translator.

01:16:52.690 --> 01:16:54.560
We're closing with Mitsubishi tonight.

01:16:54.650 --> 01:16:55.980
Tim Geithner's calling again.

01:16:57.280 --> 01:16:58.730
Cover your ears.

01:16:58.820 --> 01:17:02.530
You tell Tim Geithner to fucking blow me. I'm trying to save my company.

01:17:05.320 --> 01:17:08.200
Put him on the schedule, but make sure it's after lunch,

01:17:08.290 --> 01:17:11.040
because that way I can fit Madeline in beforehand. And...

01:17:12.160 --> 01:17:14.870
Morgan just made a deal with Mitsubishi.

01:17:15.250 --> 01:17:18.840
So we're the only bank with our dick still in the wind.

01:17:19.260 --> 01:17:21.720
I'm calling Buffett again.

01:17:26.430 --> 01:17:28.810
Grandpa, they keep calling. What if it's important?

01:17:29.060 --> 01:17:31.100
They always think it's important.

01:17:34.600 --> 01:17:37.400
All right, I'll make it fast.

01:17:38.570 --> 01:17:40.480
Warren, thanks for calling back. How are you?

01:17:40.570 --> 01:17:44.240
Just fine, Lloyd. What can I do for you today?

01:17:44.410 --> 01:17:46.910
I'd like to talk to you about taking a stake in Goldman Sachs.

01:17:46.990 --> 01:17:49.580
What do you have in mind? Sounds expensive.

01:17:50.450 --> 01:17:51.870
Maybe not.

01:17:54.000 --> 01:17:56.710
You better have good news,

01:17:56.790 --> 01:18:00.590
because I might be dead before we finish haggling over TARP.

01:18:01.210 --> 01:18:04.130
Buffett's throwing five billion into Goldman.

01:18:06.640 --> 01:18:08.550
We're off the ledge.

01:18:09.220 --> 01:18:11.220
For the moment.

01:18:11.390 --> 01:18:13.850
The October surprise arrived a little early,

01:18:13.930 --> 01:18:16.600
with a game-changing jolt to the White House race.

01:18:17.270 --> 01:18:20.980
Call it a master stroke or a desperate Hail Mary pass,

01:18:21.070 --> 01:18:24.150
McCain announced he would suspend his campaign

01:18:24.240 --> 01:18:27.360
and asked for this Friday's debate to be postponed

01:18:27.450 --> 01:18:30.330
so he can return to Washington to focus on the economic crisis.

01:18:30.410 --> 01:18:36.370
Now is our chance to come together to prove that Washington is once again

01:18:36.960 --> 01:18:39.460
capable of leading this country.

01:18:39.540 --> 01:18:42.300
Thank God he came back.

01:18:42.630 --> 01:18:46.590
Like Daniel Day-Lewis in Last of the Mohicans,

01:18:46.680 --> 01:18:49.050
he will find us!

01:18:51.430 --> 01:18:53.310
- They're blowing it up.  - What happened?

01:18:53.390 --> 01:18:56.480
McCain wanted to swoop in and save the day, but doesn't have any idea how to do it.

01:18:56.560 --> 01:19:00.690
It's a 100-page piece of legislation we've been carefully negotiating for days.

01:19:00.770 --> 01:19:02.900
What, did he think they were gonna sit around and spitball?

01:19:02.980 --> 01:19:04.820
Yeah, when McCain got here

01:19:04.900 --> 01:19:08.530
and found out that the deal was basically done, he went apeshit.

01:19:08.610 --> 01:19:11.910
The Republicans fell in behind their guy. They wanna start all over again.

01:19:11.990 --> 01:19:13.910
Democrats flipped out. Republicans dug in.

01:19:14.330 --> 01:19:16.250
- Clusterfuck.  - Clusterfuck.

01:19:16.830 --> 01:19:19.580
- Where's Hank?  - Still talking to McCain.

01:19:20.040 --> 01:19:22.340
If you or anyone else does anything

01:19:22.420 --> 01:19:26.420
that causes the next depression, it is not gonna be on me.

01:19:26.510 --> 01:19:27.760
I have a big mouth, Senator,

01:19:27.840 --> 01:19:29.380
- a very big mouth.  - Hold on...

01:19:29.470 --> 01:19:34.350
He's not threatening the Republican presidential nominee, is he?

01:19:34.430 --> 01:19:37.430
- Something's gotta be done.  - No, no. Excuse me, please.

01:19:37.520 --> 01:19:38.850
Where are the Democrats?

01:19:38.940 --> 01:19:40.690
They're right down this way.

01:19:40.770 --> 01:19:42.190
- Is Nancy there?  - Yes. Yes, she is.

01:19:42.270 --> 01:19:43.400
Thank you.

01:19:51.740 --> 01:19:53.070
Nancy...

01:19:57.410 --> 01:20:01.080
Oh, Gee, Hank. I didn't know you were Catholic.

01:20:02.920 --> 01:20:05.300
I beg of you, don't blow this up. It was a bad meeting,

01:20:05.380 --> 01:20:07.050
but we need to get together on this.

01:20:07.130 --> 01:20:08.970
We're not the ones trying to blow it up.

01:20:09.050 --> 01:20:11.260
I just ask you to stay with us.

01:20:13.720 --> 01:20:15.180
Please.

01:20:16.770 --> 01:20:20.230
The House began debating the rescue bill at 9:27 this morning.

01:20:20.310 --> 01:20:22.940
At about 1:28 p.m., Congress did finally act.

01:20:23.020 --> 01:20:25.440
- This is a squeaker of a vote.  - We are down to it right now.

01:20:25.520 --> 01:20:27.480
It looks like the Democrats delivered their caucus

01:20:27.570 --> 01:20:29.820
and the Republicans so far have not.

01:20:29.900 --> 01:20:32.110
What do you think they could do to change the bill

01:20:32.200 --> 01:20:33.990
to get a few Republican votes over to the other side?

01:20:34.070 --> 01:20:35.530
If they hang Wall Street CEOs,

01:20:35.620 --> 01:20:37.200
I think that would get a few more votes.

01:20:37.290 --> 01:20:40.660
I'm not sure what they could do exactly.

01:20:40.750 --> 01:20:44.580
It is possible that the leadership of the Congress would conclude

01:20:44.670 --> 01:20:47.340
that there's nothing that can get a majority of support

01:20:47.420 --> 01:20:50.420
and see what Hank Paulson can do and the administration can do.

01:20:52.340 --> 01:20:53.930
Leaders are negotiating right now

01:20:54.010 --> 01:20:56.350
who risks their seat. And that's what I'm hearing.

01:20:56.430 --> 01:20:58.180
Pelosi wouldn't have brought the bill to the floor

01:20:58.270 --> 01:20:59.350
if she didn't think it would pass.

01:20:59.430 --> 01:21:01.850
I don't know the answer to that, Charlie. I can't answer.

01:21:01.940 --> 01:21:03.060
I'm just reading an e-mail that's coming to me.

01:21:03.150 --> 01:21:06.110
I think the bottom line is they gotta go back to the drawing board.

01:21:06.190 --> 01:21:07.770
Do they have the time, though, to do that?

01:21:07.860 --> 01:21:09.360
Look at the Dow Jones Industrial Average.

01:21:09.440 --> 01:21:11.150
You know you're gonna get a sell-off.

01:21:11.240 --> 01:21:14.160
You guys, at this point, we just gotta hold on to the sides of the kayak

01:21:14.240 --> 01:21:15.280
and ride through this because...

01:21:15.370 --> 01:21:17.240
They didn't pass it.

01:21:17.330 --> 01:21:20.290
- They did not pass it.  - It just wasn't enough.

01:21:21.580 --> 01:21:24.580
They're gonna end up being nine votes short, Bill.

01:21:24.670 --> 01:21:25.710
Say that again, Steve.

01:21:25.790 --> 01:21:27.710
I think they're gonna end up being nine votes...

01:21:27.800 --> 01:21:29.590
If they get all six... Five of the remaining votes,

01:21:29.670 --> 01:21:33.300
they'll be 10 votes short now, if they get all five remaining.

01:21:33.380 --> 01:21:35.470
The writing is on the wall there.

01:21:35.550 --> 01:21:36.840
And we're here down on the floor.

01:21:36.930 --> 01:21:38.220
Of course, traders have been standing around.

01:21:38.310 --> 01:21:39.930
Look, we're all here in knots...

01:21:44.480 --> 01:21:47.480
The networks are waiting for you in the press room.

01:21:48.360 --> 01:21:49.940
I can't even...

01:22:02.830 --> 01:22:04.330
We have to go back again.

01:22:05.250 --> 01:22:06.830
They don't get to say no on this.

01:22:08.250 --> 01:22:09.460
Okay.

01:22:09.550 --> 01:22:10.630
The White House wants a meeting.

01:22:10.710 --> 01:22:12.630
Good. We'll come up with a new strategy.

01:22:12.720 --> 01:22:15.260
The White House has to push harder on the Hill.

01:22:15.340 --> 01:22:17.760
Meanwhile, we need to keep working on what we do with the money.

01:22:17.850 --> 01:22:19.100
Let's act like this is gonna work.

01:22:19.180 --> 01:22:20.930
In that case, let's talk about cash injections.

01:22:21.020 --> 01:22:23.140
- Here we go again.  - We can't nationalize the banks.

01:22:23.230 --> 01:22:24.270
It's not nationalizing...

01:22:24.350 --> 01:22:26.100
That's how Japan got itself a decade-long recession.

01:22:26.190 --> 01:22:27.190
Is that where we wanna go?

01:22:29.690 --> 01:22:30.820
What else do we have?

01:22:30.940 --> 01:22:32.030
Cash for Trash.

01:22:32.110 --> 01:22:35.110
Buying toxic assets is still our go-to move.

01:22:35.200 --> 01:22:37.780
All right. Find a way to simplify it.

01:22:38.660 --> 01:22:41.450
Dan, work something up.

01:22:41.540 --> 01:22:45.250
For discussion purposes, let's see what it would look like.

01:22:55.550 --> 01:22:58.300
For those who supported the $700 billion Wall Street bailout,

01:22:58.390 --> 01:23:00.510
it was a surprising and stunning failure

01:23:00.600 --> 01:23:02.890
when the House of Representatives voted to reject it.

01:23:03.020 --> 01:23:05.480
It's a stinging defeat for President Bush,

01:23:05.560 --> 01:23:08.230
delivered at the hands of his fellow Republicans.

01:23:08.810 --> 01:23:13.150
We put forth a plan that was big because we've got a big problem.

01:23:13.400 --> 01:23:17.240
The reality is that we're in an urgent situation.

01:23:17.320 --> 01:23:20.490
And the consequences will grow worse each day

01:23:20.570 --> 01:23:21.990
if we do not act.

01:23:22.080 --> 01:23:24.410
- I know. I keep telling...  - Draft of the new bill.

01:23:25.250 --> 01:23:26.620
Are they having any luck with it?

01:23:26.710 --> 01:23:28.460
The White House thinks they got a better shot of pushing it

01:23:28.540 --> 01:23:31.500
through the Senate side first. Hopefully they're right.

01:23:31.590 --> 01:23:33.380
They asked us to back off and let them handle it.

01:23:33.460 --> 01:23:36.510
So I'm trying to back off.

01:23:42.430 --> 01:23:44.430
Buying toxic assets isn't gonna work.

01:23:45.270 --> 01:23:47.730
- Why not?  - It's too slow.

01:23:47.810 --> 01:23:48.850
I think I've been saying that.

01:23:48.940 --> 01:23:51.190
Well, we knew it was complicated, but how slow...

01:23:51.270 --> 01:23:53.270
Two months, maybe more.

01:23:54.190 --> 01:23:55.940
How is it possible we didn't know how...

01:23:56.030 --> 01:23:58.950
I didn't ask how long it would take, okay?

01:24:00.450 --> 01:24:02.120
Can we move on?

01:24:02.780 --> 01:24:06.080
So maybe we can talk about capital injections.

01:24:06.160 --> 01:24:07.830
Dan, we really can't.

01:24:07.910 --> 01:24:10.120
I think we're gonna have to.

01:24:10.210 --> 01:24:16.130
Even if Neel could expedite the process, which seems unlikely,

01:24:16.710 --> 01:24:20.840
the system is crumbling. The banks need money now.

01:24:20.930 --> 01:24:24.390
If cash injections are the most efficient way to stabilize them,

01:24:24.470 --> 01:24:27.560
then we can't back off

01:24:27.640 --> 01:24:32.400
because we're uncomfortable being the men who nationalized a few American banks.

01:24:32.480 --> 01:24:33.810
It's faster, that's for sure.

01:24:33.900 --> 01:24:37.940
It will push us back off the precipice, yes, unfreeze the system.

01:24:39.650 --> 01:24:44.280
We give them some money and they lend it out.

01:24:49.290 --> 01:24:50.460
The motion is adopted.

01:24:50.540 --> 01:24:53.080
After rejecting the Bush Administration's TARP plan

01:24:53.170 --> 01:24:56.000
only four days ago, the House passed it today.

01:24:56.090 --> 01:24:59.380
The big question now, whether investors will take comfort in the news.

01:24:59.470 --> 01:25:03.050
Current indicators show the stock market is continuing its free fall.

01:25:22.860 --> 01:25:24.160
Hello?

01:25:24.240 --> 01:25:26.530
Warren, it's Hank.

01:25:27.200 --> 01:25:29.830
I'm sorry. It's late.

01:25:31.000 --> 01:25:32.370
Am I waking you?

01:25:32.460 --> 01:25:33.960
No, not at all.

01:25:35.000 --> 01:25:37.080
Can I pick your brain for a minute?

01:25:37.170 --> 01:25:39.380
Sure. What's the problem?

01:25:40.300 --> 01:25:45.470
How do you get a healthy bank to accept a capital injection?

01:26:39.940 --> 01:26:42.780
I had to drag my ass all the way here from California.

01:26:42.860 --> 01:26:44.320
They wouldn't even tell me why.

01:26:44.860 --> 01:26:46.110
Nobody knows what this is about?

01:26:46.950 --> 01:26:48.320
Nope.

01:26:53.830 --> 01:26:55.250
Talk to Jamie?

01:26:55.330 --> 01:26:57.460
Yep. He'll be fine.

01:26:58.000 --> 01:26:59.380
Did you tell him what was coming?

01:26:59.460 --> 01:27:01.540
Uh, did I tell him we're about to make him swallow

01:27:01.630 --> 01:27:04.510
a capital injection he doesn't need? No.

01:27:05.670 --> 01:27:08.260
I, uh... I told him we need his help

01:27:08.340 --> 01:27:10.800
and I asked him very politely not to fuck with us today.

01:27:12.470 --> 01:27:13.560
He got it.

01:27:15.890 --> 01:27:16.980
She's here.

01:27:22.610 --> 01:27:25.860
Gentlemen. We think this is gonna work?

01:27:25.940 --> 01:27:28.070
Oh, yeah. They're gonna love it.

01:27:50.720 --> 01:27:54.430
We are of the view that the United States needs

01:27:54.510 --> 01:27:57.640
to take strong, decisive action

01:27:59.230 --> 01:28:02.310
to arrest the stress in our financial system.

01:28:03.940 --> 01:28:06.530
Through our new TARP authority,

01:28:07.030 --> 01:28:11.360
Treasury will purchase preferred stock

01:28:12.530 --> 01:28:14.830
in each of the banks represented in this room.

01:28:17.370 --> 01:28:19.910
That stock will yield 5 % the first five years,

01:28:20.000 --> 01:28:21.500
9 % after that.

01:28:22.920 --> 01:28:25.630
We give you money. You lend it out.

01:28:25.710 --> 01:28:30.260
It will unfreeze credit, stabilize the banks, restore confidence.

01:28:31.220 --> 01:28:34.930
Everyone will be better off with more capital in the system.

01:28:35.010 --> 01:28:38.390
And that is why all nine of you

01:28:41.190 --> 01:28:43.560
will participate in the program.

01:28:47.400 --> 01:28:48.900
Some of us don't need any capital.

01:28:49.070 --> 01:28:50.900
You all need capital.

01:28:51.570 --> 01:28:53.990
You have no idea what the market's gonna look like in a year.

01:28:54.120 --> 01:28:55.780
Neither do you.

01:28:56.910 --> 01:29:00.160
I'm not one of you New York guys with your fancy products.

01:29:00.250 --> 01:29:03.290
Why am I in this room talking about bailing you out?

01:29:03.380 --> 01:29:06.670
The stronger banks will serve as cover for the weaker banks.

01:29:06.750 --> 01:29:09.340
If just some of you take it, the money's a signal

01:29:09.420 --> 01:29:12.430
that a bank is too weak to survive. The market will devour it.

01:29:13.720 --> 01:29:15.140
How much are we talking about?

01:29:15.220 --> 01:29:16.890
A hundred and twenty-five billion.

01:29:20.430 --> 01:29:22.180
Bank of America, 15 billion.

01:29:22.270 --> 01:29:24.900
Bank of New York Mellon, three billion.

01:29:25.690 --> 01:29:28.110
Citigroup, 25 billion.

01:29:28.940 --> 01:29:31.440
Goldman Sachs, 10 billion.

01:29:31.950 --> 01:29:34.160
JPMorgan, 25 billion.

01:29:34.820 --> 01:29:36.950
Merrill Lynch, 10 billion.

01:29:37.580 --> 01:29:39.700
Morgan Stanley, 10 billion.

01:29:40.250 --> 01:29:42.290
State Street, two billion.

01:29:42.910 --> 01:29:45.250
Wells Fargo, 25 billion.

01:29:51.300 --> 01:29:54.840
What happened to buying toxic assets?

01:29:55.300 --> 01:29:57.760
- Wasn't that the idea?  - It's too slow.

01:29:57.970 --> 01:29:59.850
It'll be dead before we sort it out.

01:29:59.970 --> 01:30:03.350
So, we're just handing the reins over to you?

01:30:03.480 --> 01:30:05.390
The deal's actually pretty good.

01:30:05.480 --> 01:30:07.730
You turn your nose up at this now and we have to rescue you later,

01:30:07.810 --> 01:30:09.440
the terms won't be nearly as attractive.

01:30:09.650 --> 01:30:12.110
It's cheap capital. It's not the worst idea in the world.

01:30:12.320 --> 01:30:14.150
It is for those of us who already have capital.

01:30:14.240 --> 01:30:18.660
We will be non-voting shareholders. This will be a temporary measure.

01:30:21.540 --> 01:30:23.370
What if we decline to be involved?

01:30:24.250 --> 01:30:27.000
The Chairman of the FDIC is sitting right there.

01:30:27.670 --> 01:30:28.670
Tomorrow you'll find out

01:30:28.750 --> 01:30:31.000
that you're not as well capitalized as you think you are.

01:30:31.170 --> 01:30:33.210
What kind of protections can you offer on changes

01:30:33.300 --> 01:30:34.590
for compensation policy?

01:30:34.670 --> 01:30:38.260
I mean, if the government is going to become a part-owner,

01:30:38.350 --> 01:30:42.180
does that mean that the government is going to dictate compensation?

01:30:42.270 --> 01:30:44.350
Are you asking me about your bonus right now, John?

01:30:44.480 --> 01:30:46.520
I'm running a company.

01:30:47.190 --> 01:30:51.110
If the Fed tells me I can't offer competitive pay for talent,

01:30:51.190 --> 01:30:54.360
you're gonna launch the biggest brain drain this country's ever seen.

01:30:54.440 --> 01:30:56.110
Is that the way you wanna run the banks?

01:30:56.200 --> 01:30:58.570
Until the money is repaid to the Treasury,

01:30:58.660 --> 01:31:01.660
there will be some limitations on tax deductions and golden parachutes.

01:31:01.740 --> 01:31:03.580
- Well, there you go.  - I don't really understand

01:31:03.660 --> 01:31:06.910
why there needs to be so much tension about this.

01:31:07.210 --> 01:31:10.840
The country is facing the worst economy since the Great Depression.

01:31:10.920 --> 01:31:16.130
If the financial system collapses, it will take every one of you down.

01:31:24.680 --> 01:31:26.390
Give me the papers.

01:31:34.230 --> 01:31:35.570
- You can't just sign it.  - Oh, no?

01:31:35.650 --> 01:31:39.240
- Not without your board.  - My board's on 24-hour notice.

01:31:39.320 --> 01:31:41.700
I think they'll go along with it.

01:31:41.780 --> 01:31:45.450
And if they don't, they'll fire me.

01:31:52.090 --> 01:31:53.670
Anyone else?

01:31:59.720 --> 01:32:01.590
Are they gonna do it?

01:32:02.430 --> 01:32:05.100
They're calling their boards.

01:32:05.180 --> 01:32:07.350
- That's a good sign.  - If they do go along with this,

01:32:07.430 --> 01:32:09.690
we should announce something alongside the injections, uh,

01:32:09.770 --> 01:32:13.020
maybe a program to buy up mortgages.

01:32:13.110 --> 01:32:14.690
I'm not rushing out some half-assed plan

01:32:14.770 --> 01:32:16.570
just to make it look like we're doing something.

01:32:16.650 --> 01:32:20.070
Maybe we just beef up the restrictions on how they spend the money,

01:32:21.910 --> 01:32:23.660
something to take the stink off.

01:32:23.740 --> 01:32:26.160
Put more restrictions on it, they won't take it.

01:32:31.500 --> 01:32:33.710
One thing too many for today.

01:32:35.800 --> 01:32:39.050
They almost bring down the US economy as we know it,

01:32:39.130 --> 01:32:41.550
but we can't put restrictions on how they spend

01:32:41.640 --> 01:32:44.800
the $125 billion we're giving them

01:32:44.890 --> 01:32:48.140
because they might not take it.

01:33:20.630 --> 01:33:22.130
What's this?

01:33:23.590 --> 01:33:25.220
That is, um,

01:33:26.850 --> 01:33:28.680
a poison dart frog.

01:33:29.430 --> 01:33:32.520
Wendy took it in Guyana.

01:33:45.780 --> 01:33:47.320
Is this yours?

01:33:52.960 --> 01:33:54.670
Al Capone's.

01:33:57.000 --> 01:33:58.380
Eight out of nine are in.

01:33:58.460 --> 01:34:01.010
- B of A is still waiting on its board, but...  - It's gonna go through.

01:34:03.800 --> 01:34:05.550
I'll call the Speaker's office.

01:34:05.640 --> 01:34:08.510
And, uh, we're gonna get you on the phone with the President.

01:34:08.760 --> 01:34:10.350
Okay.

01:34:14.270 --> 01:34:19.480
Dow is up 900 points just because we were meeting.

01:34:22.280 --> 01:34:24.400
Well, that's good.

01:34:26.030 --> 01:34:27.910
That's good.

01:34:29.030 --> 01:34:31.080
It was the right thing.

01:34:32.410 --> 01:34:34.000
Absolutely.

01:34:34.120 --> 01:34:36.040
I was prepared for more blowback.

01:34:37.710 --> 01:34:41.500
They didn't have a choice. They knew that.

01:34:43.420 --> 01:34:46.510
Well, I certainly hope that they...

01:34:47.840 --> 01:34:48.890
What?

01:34:51.310 --> 01:34:55.060
I hope they use the money the way we're asking them to.

01:34:55.480 --> 01:34:59.810
They will lend it out, won't they?

01:35:02.030 --> 01:35:17.790
Of course they will.
